window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["jammu-2013"] = {"id": "jammu-2013", "title": "University of Jammu 2013", "fullTitle": "University of Jammu M.A. Psychology entrance 2013, booklet code CMN-45540-A (real paper)", "sub": "60 real questions · 2013 · no official key, every answer worked out from standard texts · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "When the researcher himself does not manipulate the independent variable but studies the variations in the independent variable due to natural settings, he is using ______ method.", "options": {"a": "Experimental method", "b": "Case study method", "c": "Participatory observation method", "d": "Systematic observation method"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: when a researcher does not manipulate the independent variable but studies how it varies naturally, the method among these options is systematic observation. In an experiment the researcher deliberately changes the independent variable (IV) and controls other factors to see its effect on the dependent variable. In non-experimental research the researcher leaves the world as it is and records how variables already differ. Systematic (structured) observation does this with a plan fixed in advance: which behaviours to record, when, where and how to code them, so that different observers record the same thing. It is often carried out in natural settings (naturalistic observation), such as a playground or a classroom. Example: Meera wants to know whether crowding in school buses goes with quarrelling. She cannot crowd children on purpose, so she rides different buses and codes every quarrel on a checklist, noting how full each bus is. Crowding varies naturally; she only records it. Option d is right because systematic observation studies behaviour as it occurs in natural settings, with a planned recording scheme and no manipulation. Exam trap: participant observation and case studies also avoid manipulation, so 'no manipulation' alone does not decide the item; participant observation names the observer's role and a case study names its scope (one person or unit), while systematic observation names a planned way of recording behaviour as it varies in natural settings. Key note: none of the options names 'natural experiment' or 'correlational method', which fit the stem most exactly; systematic observation is the only non-manipulating method offered that studies variables as they vary in natural settings, so d stands.", "topic": "Systematic observation in natural settings", "fig": ""}, {"q": "\"Each of us have a potential to be a loving person if only we would recognize it\" explains:", "options": {"a": "Biological Approach", "b": "Psychodynamic Approach", "c": "Behavioural Approach", "d": "Humanistic Approach"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: the statement that each of us has the potential to be a loving person, if only we would recognise it, expresses the humanistic approach. Humanistic psychology grew in the 1950s and 1960s as a 'third force' against psychoanalysis and behaviourism. Its leaders, Abraham Maslow (1954, Motivation and Personality) and Carl Rogers (1951, Client-Centered Therapy), held that people are basically good, have free will and carry an inborn tendency to grow towards their full potential, which Maslow called self-actualisation. Problems arise when that potential is blocked, for example when love is given only on conditions (Rogers's conditions of worth), so that people lose touch with their real selves. The task of therapy is to help the person recognise and use the potential already within. Example: Kabir believes he is cold and unlikeable. A humanistic counsellor would not dig for childhood conflicts or reward new habits; she would offer warmth, empathy and unconditional positive regard so that Kabir can discover the caring person he already is. Option d is right because the stem stresses inner potential, choice and growth, the core ideas of humanistic psychology. Exam trap: words such as 'potential', 'growth', 'free will' and 'self' signal humanistic; 'unconscious' signals psychodynamic, 'learned' or 'reinforced' signals behavioural, and 'genes', 'brain' or 'hormones' signal biological.", "topic": "Humanistic approach", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Match the following: I. Structuralism, II. Humanistic, III. Behaviourism, IV. Psychoanalysis. (a) Free will, (b) Conditioning, (c) Introspection, (d) Unconscious.", "options": {"a": "I-d, II-c, III-b, IV-a", "b": "I-c, II-a, III-d, IV-b", "c": "I-c, II-a, III-b, IV-d", "d": "I-a, II-d, III-b, IV-c"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: structuralism goes with introspection, humanistic psychology with free will, behaviourism with conditioning and psychoanalysis with the unconscious (I-c, II-a, III-b, IV-d). Each early school of psychology is remembered by one key idea or method. Structuralism grew from the laboratory of Wilhelm Wundt (Leipzig, 1879) and was named and developed by Edward Titchener (1898); it tried to break conscious experience into its basic elements (sensations, images, feelings) using trained introspection, careful self-observation and report. Psychoanalysis, the school of Sigmund Freud (1900), held that behaviour is driven by unconscious wishes and conflicts. Behaviourism, launched by John B. Watson (1913) and extended by B. F. Skinner (1938), studied only observable behaviour learned through classical and operant conditioning. Humanistic psychology, led by Abraham Maslow and Carl Rogers in the 1950s and 1960s, stressed free will and the drive to self-actualise. A quick way to solve match items is to fix the surest pair first: Arjun knows behaviourism means conditioning (III-b), which removes option b; then structuralism with introspection (I-c) removes a and d. Option c is right because it is the only option that pairs all four schools with their defining idea. Exam trap: option b gets structuralism and humanistic psychology right but swaps the last two, linking behaviourism with the unconscious, which Watson rejected outright; match items are built to look half correct.", "topic": "Schools of psychology matched", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Incorporation of new information into one's existing knowledge is called:", "options": {"a": "Assimilation", "b": "Accommodation", "c": "Imprinting", "d": "Egocentrism"}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: taking new information into one's existing knowledge is assimilation. Jean Piaget (1936, The Origins of Intelligence in Children) described thinking as built from schemas (schemes), organised patterns of knowledge or action such as 'things I can suck' or 'animals'. Adaptation to the world happens in two complementary ways. In assimilation, a new object or experience is fitted into a schema that already exists, without changing the schema. In accommodation, the schema itself is changed, or a new one is created, because the new information does not fit. Equilibration is the drive to balance the two: when assimilation fails, the child feels disequilibrium and accommodates. Example: two-year-old Riya has the schema 'dog: four legs, furry'. Seeing a calf, she points and says 'dog'. She has assimilated the calf into her dog schema. When her father explains that it is a calf that says 'moo', Riya builds a new schema, which is accommodation. Option a is right because incorporating new information into existing knowledge, without altering that knowledge, is the definition of assimilation. Exam trap: assimilation and accommodation are a pair and easily reversed; remember that assimilation changes the information to fit the schema, while accommodation changes the schema to fit the information.", "topic": "Piaget's assimilation", "fig": "Jean Piaget"}, {"q": "Learning based on consequences of responding is:", "options": {"a": "Classical conditioning", "b": "Operant conditioning", "c": "Insight learning", "d": "Observational learning"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: learning that depends on the consequences of responding is operant conditioning. In operant (instrumental) conditioning, an organism acts on its environment, and what follows the action decides whether it will be repeated. Consequences that strengthen a behaviour are reinforcers; consequences that weaken it are punishers. Edward Thorndike (1898) put cats in puzzle boxes and found that the movements which opened the door and earned food were 'stamped in' while useless movements dropped out, his law of effect. B. F. Skinner (1938) named the process operant conditioning and studied it with rats and pigeons in the operant chamber (Skinner box), where lever presses were followed by food. Positive reinforcement adds something pleasant, negative reinforcement removes something unpleasant, and both raise the behaviour; punishment lowers it. Example: Arjun's teacher gives him a star each time he hands in homework on time, and his on-time homework rises. If the stars stop for good, the behaviour gradually extinguishes. Option b is right because only operant conditioning defines learning by what happens after the response. Exam trap: in classical conditioning the learner also ends up responding, but the response is triggered by a stimulus that comes before it, not shaped by what follows it; 'consequences' always points to operant learning.", "topic": "Operant conditioning", "fig": "B. F. Skinner"}, {"q": "How many digits an individual can report back in order after a single presentation as reported by George Miller (1956)?", "options": {"a": "9±2", "b": "7±2", "c": "9±3", "d": "5±3"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: George A. Miller (1956) reported that people can repeat back about 7 plus or minus 2 items in order after a single presentation. In his famous paper 'The Magical Number Seven, Plus or Minus Two', Miller reviewed studies of immediate memory span: a list of digits, letters or words is presented once, and the person must repeat it in the correct order. Most adults manage about seven items, with a normal range of 5 to 9. This is the capacity of short-term memory, the temporary store in the multi-store model of Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968). Miller also showed that the limit is in units, not raw items: by chunking, grouping items into meaningful units, we hold far more. Example: Meera cannot recall the twelve digits 199819472001 after one hearing, but as three years (1998, 1947, 2001) she holds them easily, because twelve items have become three chunks. Later, Nelson Cowan (2001) argued that the true limit, once chunking and rehearsal are prevented, is nearer four chunks. Option b is right because 7 plus or minus 2 is the exact figure in Miller's title and findings. Exam trap: the 'plus or minus two' is part of the answer; options such as 9 plus or minus 2 or 5 plus or minus 3 are built to look like Miller's number.", "topic": "Miller's short-term memory span", "fig": "George A. Miller"}, {"q": "An aroused state that occurs within an individual because of physiological need:", "options": {"a": "Need", "b": "Drive", "c": "Motive", "d": "Instinct"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: an aroused, tense state that arises within an individual because of a physiological need is called a drive. A need is a deficiency in the body, such as a lack of food, water or sleep. The need produces a drive, a state of arousal and discomfort that energises behaviour and directs it towards a goal that will reduce the need. When the goal is reached, the need is met and the drive falls. Textbooks give the chain as need, then drive, then goal-directed behaviour, then need reduction. Clark Hull (1943, Principles of Behavior) made this the centre of his drive-reduction theory: behaviour that reduces a drive is reinforced and learned. Example: Kabir skips lunch while studying. His body's lack of food is the need; the restless, hungry feeling that pulls him from his books to the kitchen is the hunger drive; eating reduces it. Option b is right because the stem names the aroused state, not the deficiency behind it, and that aroused state is the drive. Exam trap: need and drive are a cause-and-effect pair; the need is the bodily lack, while the drive is the psychological arousal it produces, so swapping them is the commonest error.", "topic": "Need, drive and motive", "fig": ""}, {"q": "When a reinforcer works naturally without any prior special training of making contingent for occurrence of any response, it is:", "options": {"a": "Primary reinforcer", "b": "Secondary reinforcer", "c": "Continuous reinforcer", "d": "Fixed-interval reinforcer"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: a reinforcer that works naturally, without any prior training or pairing, is a primary reinforcer. A reinforcer is any consequence that increases the chance of the behaviour it follows. Primary (unconditioned) reinforcers satisfy a biological need, so they work without any learning: food for a hungry animal, water when thirsty, warmth, sleep and relief from pain. Secondary (conditioned) reinforcers, such as money, praise, grades and tokens, start out neutral and gain their power only by being repeatedly paired with primary reinforcers. Money is called a generalised reinforcer because it is linked with many primary reinforcers. B. F. Skinner (1938) used food pellets, a primary reinforcer, to train rats in the operant chamber. Example: baby Arjun stops crying when fed; milk needs no teaching to work. A gold star from his teacher years later works only because Arjun has learned what stars stand for, approval and rewards. Option a is right because the stem's key words, 'works naturally without prior training', define a primary reinforcer. Exam trap: 'continuous' and 'fixed-interval' sound like kinds of reinforcer, but they are schedules of reinforcement, rules about when reinforcement is given, described by Charles Ferster and B. F. Skinner (1957).", "topic": "Primary vs secondary reinforcers", "fig": ""}, {"q": "James-Lange theory states that:", "options": {"a": "Emotion and physiological reactions occur simultaneously", "b": "Emotion leads to physiological reactions", "c": "Emotion takes place after physiological reactions", "d": "Arousal is diffuse and not tied to specific emotions"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the James-Lange theory states that emotion follows the physiological reaction. William James (1884) and the Danish physician Carl Lange (1885) proposed it independently. Common sense says we see a bear, feel afraid and then tremble and run. James reversed the order: an emotional stimulus directly triggers bodily changes (racing heart, trembling, running), and the felt emotion is our perception of those changes. In his words, we feel sorry because we cry and afraid because we tremble. Each emotion should therefore have its own pattern of bodily response. Example: Riya hears a loud crash at night. Her heart pounds and her hands shake first; according to James-Lange, noticing that pounding and shaking is what makes her feel fear. Walter Cannon (1927) attacked the theory: the internal organs react too slowly and too similarly across emotions, and cutting their feedback to the brain in animals did not abolish emotional behaviour. Option c is right because the theory's defining claim is the sequence: stimulus, bodily reaction, then emotion. Exam trap: the simultaneous view (option a) is the Cannon-Bard theory, built to oppose James-Lange, so examiners pair them to test whether you know which comes first.", "topic": "James-Lange theory of emotion", "fig": "William James and Carl Lange"}, {"q": "The concept of primary mental abilities was given by:", "options": {"a": "Spearman", "b": "Thurstone", "c": "Guilford", "d": "Piaget"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: the concept of primary mental abilities was given by Louis Leon Thurstone (1938). Earlier, Charles Spearman (1904) had found that scores on different mental tests all correlate positively, and explained this by one general factor, g, plus specific factors, s. Thurstone, at the University of Chicago, gave a large battery of tests to students and analysed the scores with his own method of multiple factor analysis. Instead of one dominant g, he found about seven fairly separate group factors, which he called primary mental abilities: verbal comprehension, word fluency, number, space, associative memory, perceptual speed and reasoning (induction). He argued that a person is better described by a profile across these abilities than by a single IQ. Example: Kabir may be strong in number and space but weak in word fluency, while his sister Meera shows the opposite profile, though both earn the same overall IQ. Later work found that the seven abilities themselves correlate, so g returned at the top of hierarchical models. Option b is right because the term primary mental abilities comes from Thurstone's 1938 monograph of that name. Exam trap: Spearman also used factor analysis, so a student who remembers only 'factor theory' may pick him; Spearman's answer was g and s, Thurstone's was several primary abilities.", "topic": "Thurstone's primary mental abilities", "fig": "Louis Leon Thurstone"}, {"q": "When a person is trying to find a large number of solutions which fit certain criteria instead of deducing a single answer to the question, he/she is using:", "options": {"a": "Convergent thinking", "b": "Divergent thinking", "c": "Evaluation", "d": "Transformation"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: searching for many solutions that fit certain criteria, instead of deducing one correct answer, is divergent thinking. J. P. Guilford (1950), in his presidential address to the American Psychological Association, urged psychologists to study creativity, and in his Structure of Intellect model he separated two kinds of productive thinking. Convergent thinking (convergent production) narrows down to the single best or correct answer, as most IQ and multiple-choice items demand. Divergent thinking (divergent production) branches out to produce many varied answers. It is the core of creativity tests, scored for fluency (number of ideas), flexibility (variety of categories), originality (rarity of ideas) and elaboration (detail). The classic task is unusual uses: 'List all the uses you can think of for a brick.' Example: given that task, Riya lists a doorstop, a paperweight, a step to reach a high shelf, a pestle for crushing spices and a cricket wicket marker, which shows high fluency and flexibility. Ellis Paul Torrance (1966) built the Torrance Tests of Creative Thinking on the same idea. Option b is right because the stem describes generating many fitting answers, the definition of divergent production. Exam trap: convergent and divergent are a mirror pair; converge means to come together on one answer, diverge means to spread out to many.", "topic": "Divergent vs convergent thinking", "fig": "J. P. Guilford"}, {"q": "The term IQ was devised by:", "options": {"a": "Alfred Binet", "b": "David Wechsler", "c": "Lewis Terman", "d": "William Stern"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: the term IQ (intelligence quotient) was devised by the German psychologist William Stern (1912). Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905) had built the first practical intelligence test, ordering items by the age at which typical children pass them; a child's mental age (MA) was the age level of the items passed. But a two-year lag means something different at age 5 and at age 15. Stern solved this by dividing mental age by chronological age (CA), giving a quotient, the Intelligenzquotient. Lewis Terman (1916), in the Stanford-Binet, multiplied it by 100 to remove decimals and popularised the short form IQ: IQ = (MA ÷ CA) × 100. Example: Arjun is 10 years old with a mental age of 12, so his ratio IQ is 12 ÷ 10 × 100 = 120. Because the ratio fails for adults, whose mental age stops rising, David Wechsler (1939) replaced it with the deviation IQ (mean 100, standard deviation 15). Option d is right because the quotient and its name come from Stern. Exam trap: Terman is the tempting wrong choice, because he added the × 100 and made 'IQ' famous through the Stanford-Binet. Key note: some books credit Terman because he made 'IQ' the standard term and added the × 100; the quotient itself is Stern's, so d stands.", "topic": "Origin of the term IQ", "fig": "William Stern"}, {"q": "The method of social psychology that involves asking all group members to identify specific persons within the group they would prefer to have as partners in a given activity:", "options": {"a": "Survey method", "b": "Interview method", "c": "Observation method", "d": "Sociometry method"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d: asking every member of a group whom they would prefer as a partner for a given activity is the sociometric method (sociometry). Jacob L. Moreno (1934, Who Shall Survive?) developed it to measure the pattern of attractions and rejections within a group. Each member privately names, say, two or three people they would most like to work, sit or play with for a stated purpose, and sometimes those they would not. The choices are counted and drawn as a sociogram, a diagram of circles joined by arrows. It shows stars (chosen by many), isolates (chosen by nobody), mutual pairs (who choose each other), chains and cliques (small sub-groups choosing only within themselves). Example: Meera, a class teacher, asks each student, 'With whom would you most like to do the science project?' The sociogram shows Kabir chosen by eleven classmates (a star) and Riya chosen by none (an isolate), so Meera pairs Riya with a kind, popular partner. Teachers, counsellors and managers use sociometry to find isolated children and to plan teams. Option d is right because the stem describes exactly the sociometric choice procedure. Exam trap: a sociometric test is filled in like a short questionnaire, so it can look like a survey, but a survey samples opinions on a topic, while sociometry asks every member to choose other members of the same group.", "topic": "Sociometry", "fig": "Jacob L. Moreno"}, {"q": "\"Personality is formed as a result of social influences\" depicts:", "options": {"a": "Socialization", "b": "Interpersonal attraction", "c": "Prosocial behaviour", "d": "Attitude"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: the idea that personality is formed by social influences describes socialisation. Socialisation is the lifelong process through which a person learns the language, norms, values, roles and habits of their society and so becomes a functioning member of it. A newborn has inherited tendencies, but who the child becomes (polite or rude, religious or not, competitive or cooperative) is shaped by agents of socialisation: the family first (primary socialisation), then school, peers, religion and the mass media (secondary socialisation). George Herbert Mead (1934, Mind, Self and Society) argued that the self itself is social, formed by taking the role of others, first in play and later in organised games. Example: Riya grows up in a joint family where elders are greeted by touching their feet and meals are shared; at school she learns to queue and take turns; through friends she picks up the slang and music of her age group. Each agent leaves its mark on her personality. Option a is right because only socialisation names the process by which social influences shape the developing person. Exam trap: the other options are also social psychology terms, so they sound right in a 'social influences' stem; ask which one is a process of becoming a person, rather than a single behaviour or evaluation.", "topic": "Socialisation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Leadership that is preoccupied with purposes, values, morals and ethics is known as:", "options": {"a": "Transformational", "b": "Autocratic", "c": "Transactional", "d": "Democratic"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: leadership concerned with purposes, values, morals and ethics is transformational leadership. James MacGregor Burns (1978), in his book Leadership, contrasted two kinds. Transactional leadership is an exchange: the leader gives rewards (pay, praise, promotion) for performance and corrects mistakes. Transforming leadership goes further: leader and followers raise one another to higher levels of motivation and morality, so that followers come to share a purpose larger than their own self-interest. Bernard Bass (1985) developed this into transformational leadership, later summarised as four components: idealised influence (a moral role model), inspirational motivation (a compelling vision), intellectual stimulation (encouraging new ways of thinking) and individualised consideration (attention to each follower's growth). Mahatma Gandhi is often cited as a transforming leader. Example: Kabir runs an NGO. Instead of only paying bonuses for targets, he keeps reminding staff why their work in village schools matters, models honesty, invites their ideas and mentors each one; staff stay late because they believe in the mission. Option a is right because a focus on values, morals and ethics is the defining mark of transforming (transformational) leadership. Exam trap: transactional is the look-alike; it too comes from Burns (1978), but it rests on exchanging rewards for performance, not on values and ethics.", "topic": "Transformational leadership", "fig": "James MacGregor Burns"}, {"q": "Which one among the following is not the principle of nonverbal communication?", "options": {"a": "Multichanneled", "b": "Culture bound", "c": "Convey emotions", "d": "Use of words"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d: 'use of words' is not a principle of nonverbal communication, because communication through words is verbal by definition. Nonverbal communication is the sending and receiving of messages without words: facial expressions, eye contact, gestures, posture, touch, physical distance (proxemics, studied by Edward T. Hall in The Hidden Dimension, 1966) and paralanguage, the tone, pitch, speed and loudness of the voice. Textbooks list several principles. It is multichannelled: face, voice, body and space send signals at the same moment and are read together. It is culture bound: gestures and display rules differ across cultures, so a thumbs-up or a head wobble can mean different things in different places, although Paul Ekman and Wallace Friesen (1971) found that a few basic facial expressions of emotion are recognised worldwide. It conveys emotions and attitudes powerfully, often more honestly than words, and it is continuous and often ambiguous. Example: Arjun tells his mother 'I am fine' after his exam, but his slumped shoulders, flat voice and avoided eye contact tell her he is upset; she trusts the nonverbal channels over the words. Option d is right because words belong to verbal communication, so their use cannot be a principle of nonverbal communication. Exam trap: in a 'which is NOT' item the odd one out is often the option that contradicts the definition itself; here nonverbal literally means 'without words'.", "topic": "Principles of nonverbal communication", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The enduring mental representations of various features of the social or physical world which are acquired through experience and have direct influence on subsequent behaviour:", "options": {"a": "Prejudice", "b": "Attitudes", "c": "Socialization", "d": "Interpersonal attraction"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: enduring mental representations of features of the social or physical world, acquired through experience and influencing later behaviour, are attitudes. An attitude is a learned, fairly lasting evaluation of an attitude object, which may be a person, group, thing, place or idea, along a like-dislike dimension. The stem's textbook definition stresses three features: attitudes last (they are enduring), they are learned from experience (direct contact, parents, peers, media), and they guide what we later do. Gordon Allport (1935) gave the classic version: 'a mental and neural state of readiness, organized through experience, exerting a directive or dynamic influence upon the individual's response to all objects and situations with which it is related.' The ABC model of Milton Rosenberg and Carl Hovland (1960) splits an attitude into affect (feelings), behaviour (action tendencies) and cognition (beliefs). Example: after years of home-cooked meals and a cousin's illness, Meera holds a firm negative attitude towards junk food: she believes it is harmful, dislikes it and walks past fast-food stalls. Option b is right because the stem lists exactly the defining features of an attitude: lasting, learned and directive. Exam trap: prejudice also fits 'learned and enduring', but it is a special, negative attitude towards a social group; the stem is general, covering any feature of the social or physical world.", "topic": "Definition of attitudes", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which component of attitude transforms prejudice into discrimination?", "options": {"a": "Affective", "b": "Cognitive", "c": "Behavioural", "d": "Emotional"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: the behavioural component of an attitude is what turns prejudice into discrimination. The ABC (tripartite) model of Milton Rosenberg and Carl Hovland (1960) divides an attitude into three parts: affective (feelings), behavioural or conative (action tendencies and actual acts) and cognitive (beliefs). Attitudes towards social groups follow the same pattern. A stereotype is the cognitive part, a belief that members of a group share certain traits. Prejudice is the attitude itself, chiefly its negative affective part (dislike, contempt), supported by stereotypes. Discrimination is the behavioural part: unfair action towards members of the group because of their membership. Prejudice stays inside the head; it becomes discrimination only when it shows in action. Example: a landlord believes students from one region are noisy (stereotype) and dislikes them (prejudice). When he refuses to rent a flat to Kabir because of where Kabir comes from, that act is discrimination. Attitudes and actions do not always match: Richard LaPiere (1934) toured the USA with a Chinese couple who were refused service only once, yet most establishments that replied to his later letter said they would not serve Chinese guests. Option c is right because discrimination is prejudice expressed in behaviour. Exam trap: 'emotional' is simply another word for affective, so a and d amount to the same answer, and neither turns feeling into action.", "topic": "Prejudice vs discrimination", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The major cause of the greenhouse effect is:", "options": {"a": "Carbon dioxide", "b": "Chlorofluorocarbons", "c": "Methane", "d": "Nitrogen"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: carbon dioxide is the major cause of the greenhouse effect among the options. Sunlight passes through the atmosphere and warms the Earth's surface, which then gives off heat as infrared radiation. Certain gases, the greenhouse gases, absorb much of this outgoing heat and send part of it back down, keeping the planet warm, much as glass keeps a greenhouse warm. Without this natural effect, Earth would be frozen. The problem is the enhanced greenhouse effect: since industrialisation, burning coal, oil and gas and cutting down forests have raised carbon dioxide (CO2) levels sharply, trapping more heat and causing global warming. CO2 is the largest single contributor to human-caused warming because it is released in huge amounts and stays in the air for centuries. Environmental psychology studies how people perceive such threats and how to change behaviour, for example by persuading people to save energy. Example: Arjun's school runs a campaign to switch off fans and lights in empty rooms and to cycle to school; each saving cuts the CO2 released by power stations and vehicles. Option a is right because carbon dioxide is the main gas behind the greenhouse effect and global warming. Exam trap: chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs) are famous for an environmental problem too, but their best-known harm is thinning the ozone layer, a different issue from the greenhouse effect. Key note: water vapour is actually the largest contributor to the natural greenhouse effect, but it is not an option, and of the options carbon dioxide is the major cause, so a stands.", "topic": "Greenhouse effect and carbon dioxide", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The Protection-Motivation theory that can be used for designing illness prevention programmes was proposed by:", "options": {"a": "Rogers", "b": "Selye", "c": "McDougall", "d": "Kurt Lewin"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: protection motivation theory was proposed by Ronald W. Rogers (1975, revised 1983), an American psychologist, not the counsellor Carl Rogers (1951). The theory explains when people act to protect themselves from a health threat, and why fear alone often fails. Protection motivation, the intention to act, depends on two appraisals. Threat appraisal asks how serious the danger is (perceived severity) and how likely it is to happen to me (perceived vulnerability). Coping appraisal asks whether the recommended action works (response efficacy), whether I can do it (self-efficacy) and what it costs (response costs). People change only when both appraisals are high: a frightening message without a clear, doable solution leads to denial or avoidance. Health campaigns use the theory to design anti-smoking, sunscreen, vaccination and condom-use programmes. Example: Kabir, a smoker, sees a warning showing lung cancer (severity) and learns that smokers his age are at risk (vulnerability). He actually quits only when the campaign also shows that quitting cuts the risk and gives him a helpline and nicotine patches (response efficacy and self-efficacy). Option a is right because the theory is Ronald W. Rogers's, and the option names only his surname. Exam trap: the bare surname 'Rogers' makes most students think of Carl Rogers and client-centred therapy; recognise the theory first, then the name fits.", "topic": "Protection motivation theory", "fig": "Ronald W. Rogers"}, {"q": "The hereditary aspects of personality such as sensitivity, irritability, distractibility, adaptability etc. are referred as:", "options": {"a": "Trait", "b": "Type", "c": "Character", "d": "Temperament"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: the hereditary aspects of personality, such as sensitivity, irritability, distractibility and adaptability, are called temperament. Temperament is a person's built-in style of emotional reacting: how active they are, how easily they are upset, how strongly they respond and how quickly they settle. It shows up in the first weeks of life, is largely inherited and stays fairly stable, so it is the raw material on which experience later builds personality. Gordon Allport (1961) named temperament, with physique and intelligence, as one of the three inherited raw materials of personality. The classic evidence is the New York Longitudinal Study begun by Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess (1956). They rated babies on nine dimensions, among them activity level, rhythmicity, adaptability, threshold of responsiveness, intensity, mood, distractibility and persistence, and found that most babies fell into three groups: easy, difficult and slow-to-warm-up. Example: baby Meera cries at every new face and startles at small sounds, while her cousin Kabir feeds and sleeps on a regular schedule and smiles at strangers. They differ in temperament long before upbringing could explain it. Option d is right because every quality in the stem is one of the inborn reaction styles that define temperament. Exam trap: trait, type and character are all personality terms, so they look plausible, but none is defined by heredity. A trait is any lasting dimension, learned or inherited; a type is a category; character is the moral, learned side. The word \"hereditary\" points to temperament.", "topic": "Temperament as inherited personality", "fig": ""}, {"q": "According to Sigmund Freud if parents take too strict approach during anal stage there is possibility of ______ personality.", "options": {"a": "Anal-expulsive", "b": "Anal-retentive", "c": "Oedipus complex", "d": "Electra complex"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: according to Sigmund Freud, parents who are too strict during the anal stage may produce an anal-retentive personality. Sigmund Freud, in Three Essays on the Theory of Sexuality (1905, with the oral, anal and phallic stages added between 1913 and 1923), described psychosexual stages in which the libido, the pleasure-seeking energy of the id, centres on one body zone at a time. In the anal stage, about 18 months to 3 years, pleasure comes from holding back and expelling faeces, and toilet training is the child's first big clash with social rules. If the conflict of a stage is badly handled, some libido stays stuck there (fixation) and colours adult personality. Harsh, early or punishing toilet training leads the child to hold back in defiance, and the adult becomes anal-retentive: orderly, stingy, obstinate and compulsively neat. In \"Character and Anal Erotism\" (1908) Freud summed these up as orderly, parsimonious and obstinate. Over-lenient training, or a child who defies by soiling, leads to the anal-expulsive pattern: messy, careless, wasteful, sometimes cruel or rebellious. Example: Arjun's mother scolded him for every accident during toilet training; as an adult he colour-codes his files, hates lending money and refuses to change his mind. Option b is right because strict toilet training is the cause Freud gave for anal-retentive fixation. Exam trap: examiners reverse the pair. Strict training gives retentive (holding on); lax training gives expulsive (letting go). The Oedipus and Electra complexes are distractors from the next stage, the phallic stage.", "topic": "Freud's anal-retentive fixation", "fig": "Sigmund Freud"}, {"q": "According to Jung ______ derives from our prehuman, animal past, when our concerns were limited to survival and reproduction.", "options": {"a": "Archetype", "b": "Persona", "c": "Shadow", "d": "Animus"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: in the theory of Carl Jung (1875 to 1961), the shadow is the archetype that derives from our prehuman, animal past, when our concerns were limited to survival and reproduction. Jung split the unconscious into two layers. The personal unconscious holds a person's own forgotten and repressed experiences. Beneath it lies the collective unconscious, inherited by all humans from the experience of their ancestors. Its contents are archetypes: universal, inborn images that shape how we react to recurring life experiences such as mother, hero, birth and death. The shadow is the darkest and most powerful archetype. It holds the primitive animal instincts inherited from our prehuman ancestors (aggression, lust, the urge to survive) and the sides of ourselves we refuse to admit. Because we deny it, we project it onto others and meet it in devils, villains and enemies. Yet Jung also saw it as a source of energy, spontaneity and creativity, and wanted it accepted, not crushed, on the way to individuation. Example: Kabir, who prides himself on being gentle, is furious at a colleague's \"aggressiveness\" while failing to notice his own hostile remarks; Jung would say Kabir is projecting his shadow. Option c is right because the shadow is the one archetype Jung tied specifically to our animal past and its survival and reproductive instincts. Exam trap: \"archetype\" is true but too broad, since the shadow is only one archetype among many. The persona is the public mask, and the anima and animus are the opposite-sex sides.", "topic": "Jung's shadow archetype", "fig": "Carl Jung"}, {"q": "______ traits tell us how people from a particular nation or culture are similar.", "options": {"a": "Central", "b": "Source", "c": "Cardinal", "d": "Common"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: common traits tell us how people from a particular nation or culture are similar. Gordon Allport (1937), a founder of trait psychology, saw a trait as a real structure inside the person that makes many situations alike for that person and guides consistent behaviour. He separated two kinds. Common traits are shared, in varying degrees, by the members of a culture, so people can be placed on the same scale and compared (how assertive, how hospitable). Individual traits, which Allport (1961) renamed personal dispositions, are unique to one person and come in three levels. A cardinal trait is so dominant that it shapes almost everything a person does (rare; \"Machiavellian\" names one). Central traits are the 5 to 10 outstanding qualities you would write in a reference letter. Secondary traits appear only in some situations (a liking for spicy food). Example: a researcher compares how hospitable Indian students are with students from another country; hospitality there is treated as a common trait. Riya's own blend of warmth, wit and stubbornness is her set of personal dispositions. Option d is right because only common traits are defined by being shared across a cultural group, which is what lets us compare one nation with another. Exam trap: cardinal, central and secondary traits all describe one individual, not a group, and source traits belong to Raymond Cattell (16PF, 1949), not Allport. Cattell also spoke of common and unique traits, but the culture-based definition in this stem is Allport's.", "topic": "Allport's common traits", "fig": "Gordon Allport"}, {"q": "According to DSM-IV-TR classification \"Personality Disorders and Mental Retardation\" are in:", "options": {"a": "Axis-I", "b": "Axis-II", "c": "Axis-III", "d": "Axis-IV"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: in DSM-IV-TR, personality disorders and mental retardation were coded on Axis II. The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, published by the American Psychiatric Association, used a multiaxial system from DSM-III (1980) to DSM-IV-TR (2000). Each patient was described on five axes so that clinicians would look beyond the main complaint. Axis I: clinical disorders, such as schizophrenia, major depression and anxiety disorders, plus other conditions that may be a focus of clinical attention. Axis II: personality disorders and mental retardation, long-standing patterns that begin early and colour the whole clinical picture. Axis III: general medical conditions (diabetes, hypothyroidism). Axis IV: psychosocial and environmental problems (job loss, divorce). Axis V: the Global Assessment of Functioning (GAF), a rating from 0 to 100. Giving personality disorders and mental retardation their own axis made sure they were not overlooked beside a more dramatic Axis I disorder. Example: Riya is admitted with major depression (Axis I); she also has borderline personality disorder (Axis II), asthma (Axis III) and recent unemployment (Axis IV), and her GAF is 45 (Axis V). Option b is right because Axis II was the axis reserved for personality disorders and mental retardation. Exam trap: examiners offer Axis I, which held the clinical syndromes, or swap Axis III (medical conditions) with Axis IV (stressors). Remember too that DSM-5 (2013) dropped the axes altogether and renamed mental retardation intellectual disability.", "topic": "DSM-IV-TR multiaxial system", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Explaining mental disorders with a combination of theoretical perspectives is:", "options": {"a": "Somatogenic approach", "b": "Psychogenic approach", "c": "Multiple causality approach", "d": "Psychometric approach"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: explaining mental disorders with a combination of theoretical perspectives is the multiple causality approach. Early theories were one-dimensional: each blamed a single cause. The somatogenic view looked for bodily causes; it gained strength when Richard von Krafft-Ebing (1897) showed that general paresis, a severe mental decline, followed earlier syphilis. The psychogenic view looked for psychological causes, from the \"animal magnetism\" of Franz Anton Mesmer (1770s) to the unconscious conflicts described by Sigmund Freud and Josef Breuer (1895). Research then showed that almost no disorder has only one cause. The multiple causality (multidimensional or integrative) approach holds that biological, psychological, social and cultural factors act together. Two models express it. The biopsychosocial model of George Engel (1977) asks about all three levels at once. The diathesis-stress model says a disorder appears when an inborn or early vulnerability (the diathesis) meets enough stress. Example: Arjun becomes depressed after losing his job. A multiple causality account cites his family history of depression (biological), his habit of blaming himself (psychological) and his isolation after moving cities (social), all interacting. Option c is right because only this approach explains disorders by combining perspectives instead of choosing one. Exam trap: \"psychometric\" sounds like \"psychogenic\", but it is about measuring abilities and traits with tests, not about what causes disorders.", "topic": "Multiple causality of disorders", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In which anxiety disorder flashbacks of images, sounds, smell, feelings etc. usually occur?", "options": {"a": "Panic disorder", "b": "Obsessive-compulsive disorder", "c": "Post traumatic stress disorder", "d": "General anxiety disorder"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: flashbacks of images, sounds, smells and feelings are the hallmark of post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD). PTSD follows exposure to a traumatic event involving actual or threatened death, serious injury or sexual violence: war, assault, rape, a serious accident or a disaster. The person may have lived through it, witnessed it, or learned that it happened to someone close. DSM-5 (2013) groups the symptoms into four clusters. Intrusion: unwanted memories, nightmares and flashbacks, in which the person feels or acts as if the event were happening again, often with vivid sensory fragments (the smell of smoke, the screech of brakes). Avoidance of reminders. Negative changes in mood and thinking, such as self-blame, guilt and detachment. Hyperarousal: being jumpy, irritable, sleepless and constantly on guard. Symptoms must last more than one month; if they clear within a month, the diagnosis is acute stress disorder. Example: months after surviving a bus crash, Meera smells diesel at a petrol pump and suddenly sees the crash, hears the screams and feels her heart pound as if it were happening again. Option c is right because reliving the trauma through sensory flashbacks is the defining symptom of PTSD. Exam trap: the stem calls PTSD an anxiety disorder because it was one in DSM-IV-TR (2000); DSM-5 moved it to a new chapter, trauma- and stressor-related disorders. Its one-month rule is also tested against acute stress disorder.", "topic": "PTSD and flashbacks", "fig": ""}, {"q": "When an individual worries excessively about a variety of everyday problems for at least six months, the person may have:", "options": {"a": "General anxiety disorder", "b": "Panic disorder", "c": "Social phobia", "d": "OCD"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: excessive worry about many everyday problems for at least six months points to generalised anxiety disorder (GAD), printed here as \"General anxiety disorder\". GAD is chronic, hard-to-control worry about a number of ordinary events or activities: work, studies, health, money, family. The worry is out of proportion to the real risk, moves from topic to topic and is present more days than not. DSM-IV-TR (2000) and DSM-5 (2013) require at least six months of such worry, with at least three of six symptoms in adults (one in children): restlessness or feeling on edge, being easily tired, poor concentration, irritability, muscle tension and disturbed sleep. Because the anxiety is not tied to any one object or situation, older texts called it free-floating anxiety. Example: for the past year Kabir, a bank clerk, has worried that he will make an error at work, that his parents will fall ill, that his savings will run out and that his son will fail his exams; he is tense, sleeps badly and cannot relax even on holiday. Option a is right because GAD is defined by excessive worry about a variety of everyday matters lasting at least six months. Exam trap: the six-month rule is shared with specific phobia and social anxiety disorder (typically six months), so judge by content: diffuse worry about many things is GAD, fear of one kind of situation is a phobia, and sudden attacks are panic disorder.", "topic": "Generalised anxiety disorder", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Method used by earlier experimental psychologists in which the subjects were asked to examine their own conscious thoughts and feelings and report accordingly, is known as:", "options": {"a": "Catharsis", "b": "Introspection", "c": "Free association", "d": "Self-disclosure"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: introspection is the method in which early experimental psychologists asked subjects to examine their own conscious thoughts and feelings and report them. Introspection means \"looking within\". Wilhelm Wundt (1879), who opened the first psychology laboratory at Leipzig, trained observers to report their immediate experience of a carefully controlled stimulus, such as a tone or a flash of light, in short, precise judgements. He kept this controlled inner perception separate from casual armchair self-observation. His student Edward Bradford Titchener used a stricter version at Cornell and named the school structural psychology, or structuralism (1898); his observers broke consciousness into elements: sensations, images and feelings. They had to avoid the \"stimulus error\", naming the object (\"an apple\") instead of describing the raw experience (\"a glossy red patch\"). The method lost favour because different laboratories got different results that no one could check: the Würzburg school of Oswald Kulpe (early 1900s) reported imageless thought, which Titchener denied, and John B. Watson (1913) rejected introspection altogether. Example: in the laboratory, Riya listens to a click and reports exactly how the sound feels in quality, intensity and duration, without saying \"it is a clock\". Option b is right because introspection is exactly this systematic self-report of one's own conscious experience. Exam trap: free association also asks people to report what is in their mind, but it is the clinical technique of Sigmund Freud (1890s) for reaching the unconscious, not a laboratory method for describing conscious experience.", "topic": "Introspection in early psychology", "fig": "Wilhelm Wundt"}, {"q": "Which among the following is not the basic psychophysical method?", "options": {"a": "Method of limits", "b": "Method of constant stimuli", "c": "Method of adjustment", "d": "Method of variation"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: \"method of variation\" is not one of the basic psychophysical methods. Gustav Fechner (1860), in Elements of Psychophysics, set out three classical methods for measuring thresholds, the point at which a stimulus, or a difference between stimuli, is just detected. Method of limits (method of minimal changes): the experimenter changes the stimulus in small steps, in ascending and descending series, until the observer's report switches from \"no\" to \"yes\" or back; the threshold is the average switching point. Errors of habituation (keeping the same answer too long) and anticipation (switching too early) are balanced by alternating the series. Method of constant stimuli (method of right and wrong cases): a fixed set of stimuli near the threshold is presented many times in random order, and the threshold is the value detected on 50 per cent of trials; it is the most accurate but slowest. Method of adjustment (method of average error): the observers adjust the stimulus themselves until it just seems detectable or equal to a standard; it is the fastest but least precise. Example: to find Arjun's difference threshold for weight, a tester could raise a comparison weight step by step (limits), present seven fixed weights in random order many times (constant stimuli), or let Arjun add sand to a tin until it feels equal to a standard (adjustment). Option d is right because \"method of variation\" is not one of Fechner's three methods. Exam trap: \"variation\" sounds plausible because all three real methods vary the stimulus; and papers sometimes print the alternative names (minimal changes, right and wrong cases, average error), which are genuine, so do not mistake them for the odd one out.", "topic": "Fechner's psychophysical methods", "fig": "Gustav Fechner"}, {"q": "______ variables involve only whole numbers, units and categories.", "options": {"a": "Discrete", "b": "Continuous", "c": "Situational", "d": "Mediating"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: discrete variables take only whole numbers, separate units or categories, with no possible values in between. A variable is anything that can take different values, and by the kind of values it takes it is either discrete or continuous. A discrete (discontinuous) variable jumps from one value to the next: number of children (2 or 3, never 2.6), number of errors, family size, or categories such as gender, religion and type of school. A continuous variable can take any value along a range, including fractions, limited only by the precision of the measuring tool: height, weight, time and age. A reaction time could be 0.42 or 0.4213 seconds. A quick test: if a value between two neighbouring values is impossible, the variable is discrete. This is a separate property from the four levels of measurement of Stanley Smith Stevens (1946): a count is discrete yet can still sit on a ratio scale. Example: in Meera's village survey, the number of rooms in each house (3, 4, 5) is discrete, while the time each family takes to fetch water (12.5 minutes) is continuous. Option a is right because \"whole numbers, units and categories\" is the textbook definition of a discrete variable. Exam trap: an average of discrete data can be a fraction (2.4 children per family), but that does not make the variable continuous; and continuous data are often rounded to whole numbers (age 20) without becoming discrete.", "topic": "Discrete versus continuous variables", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The smallest detectable difference between a starting and secondary level of a particular sensory stimulus is known as:", "options": {"a": "Just noticeable difference", "b": "Difference limen", "c": "Differential threshold", "d": "All the three"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: just noticeable difference, difference limen and differential threshold are three names for the same thing, so \"All the three\" is right. When a stimulus changes, we do not notice every tiny change. The smallest change from a starting stimulus that a person can detect is the difference threshold. Because detection varies from trial to trial, it is defined as the change detected on 50 per cent of trials. It goes by three names: just noticeable difference (JND), named from the observer's side, the change that is just noticed; difference limen (DL), from the Latin limen, \"threshold\"; and differential threshold. Ernst Heinrich Weber (1834), comparing lifted weights, found that the JND is a constant fraction of the starting stimulus, not a fixed amount: Weber's law, ΔI / I = k. If the fraction for weight is 0.02, a 500 gram packet needs 10 grams more to feel heavier, but a 5 kilogram one needs 100 grams. Gustav Fechner (1860) built on this to found psychophysics. Example: Riya can tell that her 50 gram letter has become heavier only when 1 gram is added; that 1 gram is her JND, her difference limen and her differential threshold at that weight. Option d is right because each listed term names the same smallest detectable difference, so choosing any one alone leaves out two equally correct names. Exam trap: the absolute threshold (absolute limen) is the weakest stimulus detected at all, starting from nothing; the difference threshold is the smallest change in a stimulus already present.", "topic": "Difference threshold and JND", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The immediate response in the brain caused by excitation of a sensory organ is:", "options": {"a": "Sensation", "b": "Sensory analysis", "c": "Sensory coding", "d": "Sensory localization"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: sensation is the immediate response in the brain caused by excitation of a sensory organ. Every experience of the world begins as physical energy: light, sound waves, pressure, chemicals. Each sense organ has receptors, cells specialised for one form of energy (rods and cones for light, hair cells for sound). When energy excites the receptors, they convert it into neural impulses, a step called transduction. The impulses travel along sensory nerves to the brain, and the basic, raw experience that results (a flash of brightness, a tone, warmth) is sensation. Perception comes next: the brain organises and interprets sensations into meaningful objects and events, using past experience and expectation. Johannes Muller (1801 to 1858), in his doctrine of specific nerve energies, added that the kind of sensation depends on which nerve is excited, not on the stimulus: pressing on the closed eye makes you \"see\" light. Example: as Kabir walks into the kitchen, his smell receptors detect molecules in the air and he experiences a sharp, warm smell; that is sensation. Recognising it as his mother's mustard-seed tadka is perception. Option a is right because sensation is the first, immediate result of a sense organ being stimulated, before any interpretation. Exam trap: examiners mix up the steps. Transduction converts energy into impulses; sensory coding is how impulses represent intensity and quality; analysis and localisation are later processing. Illusions distort perception, not sensation.", "topic": "Sensation versus perception", "fig": ""}, {"q": "When two lights flash at a certain speed, we perceive singular light oscillating back and forth, this is:", "options": {"a": "Phi phenomenon", "b": "Pragnanz", "c": "Figural after-effect", "d": "Similarity"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: when two lights flash in turn at the right speed and we see a single light moving back and forth, this is the phi phenomenon. Max Wertheimer (1912), working in Frankfurt, flashed two lines in quick succession with a tachistoscope. With a gap of about 30 milliseconds the lines looked simultaneous; with about 200 milliseconds they looked successive; but at about 60 milliseconds a single line seemed to move from one place to the other. Nothing actually moved, so the movement could not be built up from the two separate sensations. This study of apparent motion founded Gestalt psychology, whose slogan is that the whole is different from the sum of its parts. The same effect makes films, cartoons and neon signs appear to move. Example: at a railway crossing near Arjun's school, two red lamps blink alternately, and Arjun sees one light swinging from side to side. Option a is right because the phi phenomenon is the name for this perceived movement produced by two stationary lights flashed in succession. Exam trap: Prägnanz, similarity and the figural after-effect are also Gestalt terms, so the Gestalt flavour of every option cannot help; look for the idea of movement. Key note: strictly, Wertheimer used \"phi\" for pure, objectless motion, and seeing one object travel from place to place is now called beta movement; but textbooks use \"phi phenomenon\" for this apparent motion, and beta movement is not offered, so a stands.", "topic": "Phi phenomenon and apparent motion", "fig": "Max Wertheimer"}, {"q": "Which instrument among the following is used for the measurement of reaction time?", "options": {"a": "Chronoscope", "b": "Metronome", "c": "Stereoscope", "d": "Memory drum"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: the chronoscope is the instrument used to measure reaction time. Reaction time is the interval between the onset of a stimulus and the start of the person's response. It is very short, usually about 0.15 to 0.25 seconds for a simple reaction, so early laboratories needed a precise timer. A chronoscope (\"time viewer\") is a clock that starts when the stimulus appears and stops when the subject presses a key. The Hipp chronoscope, a clockwork timer built by the clockmaker Matthaus Hipp (1840s), measured intervals to thousandths of a second and became standard equipment in the Leipzig laboratory of Wilhelm Wundt (1879). Franciscus Cornelis Donders (1868) used precise reaction timings in his subtraction method: the time of a simple reaction is subtracted from that of a choice reaction to estimate how long a mental stage, such as deciding, takes. This founded mental chronometry. Today computers do the same job. Example: Meera presses a button as soon as a light comes on, and the chronoscope shows 0.23 seconds, her simple reaction time. Option a is right because the chronoscope is the only listed instrument built to time short intervals such as reaction time. Exam trap: all four options are classic laboratory instruments, so the test is knowing each one's job; the metronome also involves time, but it beats out a rhythm instead of measuring an interval.", "topic": "Chronoscope and reaction time", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The ability of brain to merge two images coming from two eyes together is known as:", "options": {"a": "Retinal disparity", "b": "Zipper effect", "c": "Illusion", "d": "Depth perception"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: of the options given, depth perception best names the brain's ability to merge the two eyes' images, because binocular depth is what that merging produces. Our eyes are about 6 centimetres apart, so each retina receives a slightly different view of the same scene. The difference between the two images is retinal (binocular) disparity, and it is larger for near objects than for far ones. The brain combines, or fuses, the two images into a single picture and uses the disparity to work out distance. The result is stereopsis, literally \"solid seeing\": the vivid three-dimensional depth that weakens when you close one eye. Charles Wheatstone (1838) showed this with his stereoscope, which gives each eye its own flat picture; the two merge into one scene with depth. Bela Julesz (1960) used random-dot stereograms, which show no shape to either eye alone, to prove that the merging happens in the brain. Example: Kabir watches a 3D film through glasses that send a different image to each eye; his brain merges them and the dragon seems to fly out of the screen. Option d is right because merging the two eyes' images is how the brain produces binocular depth perception, the only listed ability that results from that merging. Exam trap: retinal disparity sounds right because it involves both eyes, but it is the difference between the images (the cue), not the brain's act of merging them. Key note: the exact term for merging the two images is binocular fusion, which is not printed. \"Zipper effect\" is not a standard textbook term for this; depth perception (stereopsis) is the ability that results from fusion, and retinal disparity is only the difference it works from, so d stands.", "topic": "Binocular fusion and depth", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Data representing age, weight, height are examples of ______ data, whereas data representing gender, classroom, family size are examples of ______ data:", "options": {"a": "Discrete, Continuous", "b": "Continuous, Discrete", "c": "Raw, Standard", "d": "Standard, Raw"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: age, weight and height are continuous data, whereas gender, classroom and family size are discrete data. Data are continuous when the variable can take any value within a range, including fractions, so the only limit is how finely we measure. Height can be 152 cm, 152.4 cm or 152.43 cm; weight can be 48.7 kg; age can be 19 years, 4 months and 6 days. Data are discrete when values come in separate steps, either whole-number counts or categories, with nothing possible in between. Family size is 4 or 5 people, never 4.5; gender and classroom are categories, so a student is in Class 8A or 8B, not halfway between. Continuous data are often rounded (we say \"age 20\"), but the variable is still continuous; likewise an average family size of 4.3 does not make family size continuous. The difference matters for graphs: grouped continuous data go into a histogram with touching bars, while discrete categories go into a bar chart or pie chart with separate bars or slices. Example: in Riya's class survey, each student's height in centimetres and time taken to reach school are continuous; the number of siblings and the house each student belongs to are discrete. Option b is right because the first list (age, weight, height) can take fractional values and the second (gender, classroom, family size) cannot. Exam trap: option a gives the same two words in reverse order, so check which list each blank refers to.", "topic": "Continuous and discrete data", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In which type of graphical representation the given distribution is first converted into percentage distribution?", "options": {"a": "Pictograph", "b": "Bar chart", "c": "Pie chart", "d": "Histogram"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: in a pie chart the given distribution is first converted into a percentage distribution, and the percentages are then turned into angles of a circle. A pie chart (pie diagram, circle graph) shows how one whole is divided into parts. Each category becomes a slice, or sector, whose size is proportional to its share of the total. The steps: work out each category's percentage of the total; convert each percentage to degrees, since the whole circle is 360 degrees and 1 per cent equals 3.6 degrees; then draw each sector with a protractor and label it. It suits categorical data and works best with only a few categories. William Playfair (1801) is usually credited with the first pie chart. Example: of 200 students in Arjun's college, 100 choose psychology, 60 sociology and 40 economics. As percentages these are 50, 30 and 20; as angles they are 180, 108 and 72 degrees, so the psychology slice fills half the circle. Option c is right because converting frequencies into a percentage distribution is the essential first step in drawing a pie chart. Exam trap: bar charts and histograms can also show percentages, but for them the step is optional; only the pie chart depends on it, because its slices must add up to 100 per cent of one circle.", "topic": "Pie chart construction", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which among the following is not the absolute measure of dispersion?", "options": {"a": "Range", "b": "Standard deviation", "c": "Mean deviation", "d": "Coefficient of correlation"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: the coefficient of correlation is not an absolute measure of dispersion; in fact it is not a measure of dispersion at all. Dispersion (variability) describes how spread out scores are around the centre. Absolute measures are expressed in the same units as the data (marks, kilograms, seconds). They are the range (highest minus lowest score), the quartile deviation or semi-interquartile range (half the distance between the first and third quartiles), the mean deviation (the average absolute distance of scores from the mean) and the standard deviation (the square root of the average squared deviation from the mean, the most used). Relative measures divide an absolute measure by an average to give a unit-free ratio, so groups measured in different units can be compared: the coefficient of variation of Karl Pearson (1857 to 1936), CV = (SD / Mean) x 100, and the coefficients of range and of quartile deviation. The coefficient of correlation, also Pearson's, is different in kind: it tells how strongly and in which direction two variables go together, from minus 1 to plus 1. Example: marks in Meera's class run from 35 to 85 (a range of 50 marks) with a standard deviation of 12 marks; these describe spread. A correlation of 0.6 between her classmates' study hours and marks describes a relationship. Option d is right because correlation measures the association between two variables, not the spread of one. Exam trap: the word \"coefficient\" links it to the coefficient of variation, which is a measure of dispersion, but a relative one; either way, no coefficient is an absolute measure.", "topic": "Absolute measures of dispersion", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In a symmetrical distribution Mode is equal to:", "options": {"a": "Mean", "b": "3 Median - 2 Mean", "c": "3 Mean - 2 Median", "d": "Mean - 2 Median"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: in a symmetrical distribution the mode is equal to the mean. There are three main measures of central tendency. The mean is the arithmetic average; the median is the middle score of the ordered set; the mode is the most frequent score, the peak of the curve. In a symmetrical, unimodal distribution such as the normal curve, the two halves are mirror images, so the peak, the middle point and the balance point all fall at the same place: mean = median = mode. When a distribution is skewed they separate. The mean is pulled furthest towards the long tail, the mode stays at the peak and the median lies between, so positive skew gives mode < median < mean and negative skew gives mean < median < mode. For moderately skewed distributions Karl Pearson (1857 to 1936) gave an empirical relation: Mode = 3 Median minus 2 Mean. Example: the heights of 1,000 Class 10 students in Kabir's district form a bell curve, and the average height, the middle height and the most common height are all 150 cm. Option a is right because symmetry makes the three averages coincide, so the mode equals the mean. Exam trap: option b is Pearson's genuine formula and looks like the textbook answer, but it is meant for moderately asymmetrical distributions; options c and d are distorted versions of it. Key note: in a symmetrical distribution the mean and median are equal, so b also works out to the mean, but b is the formula used for moderately asymmetrical distributions; the answer the stem asks for is that the mode equals the mean, so a stands.", "topic": "Mean, median, mode in symmetry", "fig": ""}, {"q": "From the below given sampling techniques choose the non probability technique:", "options": {"a": "Cluster sampling", "b": "Stratified sampling", "c": "Systematic sampling", "d": "Purposive sampling"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: purposive sampling is a non-probability technique, because the researcher's judgement, not chance, decides who enters the sample. In probability sampling every member of the population has a known, non-zero chance of being selected, and a random mechanism does the choosing. The main probability methods are simple random sampling, systematic sampling (every kth name from a random start), stratified sampling (dividing the population into subgroups such as gender or region and drawing randomly within each) and cluster sampling (randomly choosing whole groups, such as schools, and studying everyone in them). In non-probability sampling the chance of selection cannot be known: purposive (judgement), convenience (incidental), quota and snowball sampling. Example: Meera wants to study burnout among senior ICU nurses, so she hand-picks 20 nurses she judges experienced and typical. No other nurse had a calculable chance of being chosen, so this is purposive sampling. Option d is right because purposive sampling rests on the researcher's choice of who is relevant or typical, so each member's probability of selection is unknown. Exam trap: systematic and cluster sampling look less random than a lottery, so students pick them; but both begin with a random choice (a random starting point, randomly chosen clusters), so both remain probability methods. Key note: some Indian research texts add a third, mixed family of sampling and file systematic sampling there, because only the first unit is drawn at random and the rest follow a fixed rule; even there it is not classed as non-probability, and purposive sampling is the only non-probability technique offered, so d stands.", "topic": "Probability vs non-probability sampling", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A researcher is interested to gain familiarity with the phenomenon or to achieve new insights into it. He is doing ______ research.", "options": {"a": "Exploratory", "b": "Descriptive", "c": "Diagnostic", "d": "Historical"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: research whose aim is to gain familiarity with a phenomenon or to achieve new insights into it is exploratory (also called formulative) research. This wording comes from Claire Selltiz and her colleagues (Research Methods in Social Relations, 1959), and Indian texts such as C. R. Kothari (Research Methodology: Methods and Techniques, 2nd edition 2004) repeat it. Exploratory research is done when little is known about a topic. Its purpose is to discover, not to test: it helps the researcher formulate a precise research problem, develop hypotheses, clarify concepts and set priorities for later studies. Its methods are flexible: a survey of the literature, an experience survey (talking to people who know the field well) and the analysis of insight-stimulating cases. Example: Kabir notices that many college students describe a new kind of phone anxiety but finds almost no research on it. He interviews a few students and counsellors and reads widely to learn what the experience is like and which questions are worth asking. That is exploratory research. Option a is right because the stem's two goals, familiarity and new insights, are the textbook definition of exploratory research. Exam trap: descriptive research also studies groups we want to know more about, so it tempts; but descriptive research accurately portrays characteristics that are already defined, whereas exploratory research comes first, when even the right questions are not yet clear.", "topic": "Exploratory research", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Temperature (C, F) is measured on ______ scale.", "options": {"a": "Nominal", "b": "Ordinal", "c": "Interval", "d": "Ratio"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: temperature in degrees Celsius or Fahrenheit is measured on an interval scale. Stanley Smith Stevens (1946) described four levels of measurement. A nominal scale only names categories (gender, jersey numbers). An ordinal scale adds rank order (first, second and third in class), but the gaps between ranks need not be equal. An interval scale adds equal units: the rise from 10 to 20 degrees C is the same amount as the rise from 30 to 40 degrees C. But its zero is arbitrary, a reference point rather than the absence of heat: 0 degrees C is simply the freezing point of water. So interval values can be added and subtracted, but their ratios mean nothing. A ratio scale has equal units and a true zero, so ratios make sense (weight, height, reaction time, income, and Kelvin temperature, where 0 K is absolute zero, the true bottom of the scale). Example: Riya says that 40 degrees C in Delhi is twice as hot as 20 degrees C in Shimla. She is wrong: in Fahrenheit the same two days are 104 and 68 degrees F, a ratio of about 1.5, not 2. Option c is right because Celsius and Fahrenheit have equal intervals but no true zero, which is the defining feature of an interval scale. Exam trap: temperature looks like a precise physical measurement, so students choose ratio; only the Kelvin scale, which starts at absolute zero, is a ratio scale.", "topic": "Interval scale of measurement", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The correlation coefficient between variable X and Y is negative and significant. This indicates that:", "options": {"a": "Any increase in variable X will lead to increase in variable Y with equal proportion", "b": "Any increase in variable Y will lead to increase in variable X with equal proportion", "c": "Any increase in one variable will lead to decrease in another variable", "d": "Any increase in variable X will lead to decrease in variable Y"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: a negative correlation means the two variables move in opposite directions, so as one increases, the other tends to decrease. The correlation coefficient (Karl Pearson's r, 1896) runs from minus 1 to plus 1. Its sign gives the direction: positive when both rise together, negative when one rises as the other falls. Its size gives the strength: near 0 is weak, near 1 of either sign is strong, and only an r of exactly minus 1 or plus 1 is a perfect, equal-proportion relationship. Significant means the correlation is unlikely to be due to chance in the sample; it does not mean strong, and it never means one variable causes the other. Correlation is also symmetrical: the correlation of X with Y equals that of Y with X, so a correct statement must hold from either side. Example: Arjun finds a significant r of minus .45 between students' anxiety scores and their marks. Higher anxiety goes with lower marks, and equally, higher marks go with lower anxiety. Option c is right because it states the opposite-direction relationship for either variable, which is exactly what a negative correlation describes. Exam trap: option d also describes a negative relationship, but only from X to Y, as if X drives Y; because r is symmetrical and shows no cause, the statement about one variable and another is the complete answer. Key note: \"will lead to\" sounds causal, and correlation does not show causation; read it as \"goes with\", and c stands because a correlation runs both ways.", "topic": "Negative correlation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which among the following is not the basic requirement for a psychological test?", "options": {"a": "Objectivity", "b": "Subjectivity", "c": "Establishing norms", "d": "Standardization"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: subjectivity is not a requirement of a psychological test; it is the very thing a good test tries to remove. Anne Anastasi (Psychological Testing, 1954) defined a psychological test as essentially an objective and standardised measure of a sample of behaviour. Textbooks list the characteristics of a good test: objectivity (administration, scoring and interpretation are free from the examiner's personal judgement, so two examiners give the same score), reliability (consistent scores), validity (it measures what it claims to measure), norms (the scores of a representative reference group, against which an individual's raw score is read) and standardisation (the same instructions, time limits, materials and scoring rules for everyone), plus practicability (reasonable cost, time and ease of use). Subjectivity means the score depends on who does the scoring and this lowers both reliability and validity. Example: two teachers mark Neha's essay answer 6 and 9 out of 10 because each follows a private idea of a good answer; that is subjectivity. A multiple-choice test with a fixed scoring key would give the same score whoever marked it, which is objectivity. Option b is right because subjectivity is the opposite of objectivity and is a source of error, not a basic requirement. Exam trap: the stem is negatively worded, and objectivity and subjectivity look alike; read the question twice and pick the one quality a test designer works to eliminate.", "topic": "Characteristics of a good test", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A researcher has framed two sets of questions that measure the same concept. After collecting the responses the correlation coefficient between these two sets was calculated to check the ______ of test.", "options": {"a": "Test-retest reliability", "b": "Split-half reliability", "c": "Internal consistency", "d": "External validity"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: correlating the scores on two sets of items that measure the same concept, from one administration, checks split-half reliability. Reliability is the consistency of test scores. In the split-half method the test is given once and its items are then divided into two comparable halves, usually odd-numbered against even-numbered items. Each person's two half-scores are then correlated. Each half is only half as long as the full test, and shorter tests are less reliable, so the half-test correlation underestimates the reliability of the whole test. It is therefore corrected with the Spearman-Brown prophecy formula (Charles Spearman and William Brown, both 1910): full-test reliability = 2r / (1 + r). Example: Kabir gives his 40-item self-esteem scale once to 200 students and correlates their scores on the 20 odd items with the 20 even items. He gets r = .70, which corrects to about .82 for the full test. Option b is right because the stem describes two sets of questions on the same concept, answered at one sitting and then correlated, which is the split-half procedure. Exam trap: internal consistency is the wider family that includes split-half, so it also tempts; the specific method of correlating two sets of items is split-half. Key note: two separate sets of questions built to measure the same concept and correlated is also how parallel-forms (alternate-forms) reliability is described, and split-half is itself one kind of internal consistency; parallel forms is not an option, and the specific method of correlating two sets of items from one administration is split-half, so b stands.", "topic": "Split-half reliability", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The ability of the psychological test to make generalization of the obtained results to other settings signifies ______ validity of the test.", "options": {"a": "Content", "b": "Face", "c": "External", "d": "Concurrent"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: the extent to which results can be generalised beyond the study, to other people, settings, times and measures, is external validity. Donald T. Campbell (1957) introduced the term, and Donald T. Campbell and Julian C. Stanley (1963) made the internal and external pair standard in research design. Internal validity asks whether the study's own conclusion is sound: did the independent variable really cause the change? External validity asks whether that conclusion holds outside the study. It has two main parts: population validity (do the results apply to other groups of people?) and ecological validity (do they apply to real-life settings?). Threats include an unrepresentative sample, artificial laboratory conditions and the reactive effects of testing. Example: Ananya finds that a memory-training programme raises recall among college students in a quiet laboratory. If the same gains appear among working adults and among school children in ordinary classrooms, the finding has high external validity. Option c is right because the stem's central idea, generalising the results to other settings, is the definition of external validity. Exam trap: the stem says validity of the test, which sends students looking for a test-validity type (content, face, concurrent); but generalisability is a research-design concept, and none of those test-validity types is about other settings.", "topic": "External validity", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Consistency or uniformity of the conditions and procedures for administering the test reflect its:", "options": {"a": "Reliability", "b": "Validity", "c": "Standardization", "d": "Objectivity"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: uniformity of the conditions and procedures for giving a test is standardisation. Anne Anastasi (Psychological Testing, 1954) put it simply: standardisation implies uniformity of procedure in administering and scoring the test. Every test taker gets the same instructions, materials, time limits and practice items in similar surroundings, and every answer is scored by the same rules. The second part of standardisation is giving the test, in exactly this way, to a large representative sample whose scores become the norms. Only then is it fair to compare one person's score with another's or with the norms, because any difference should come from the person, not from how the test was given. Example: Rohan takes a reasoning test with 30 minutes and the printed instructions, while Sana takes the same test with 45 minutes and hints from the examiner. Their scores cannot be compared, because the procedure was not uniform. Option c is right because the stem defines standardisation itself: consistent conditions and procedures of administration. Exam trap: the word consistency pulls students towards reliability; but reliability is consistency of the scores (across time, forms or items), whereas standardisation is consistency of the procedure used to obtain them.", "topic": "Test standardisation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Scoring of TAT is generally based on:", "options": {"a": "Murray's personality theory", "b": "Exner's system", "c": "Cattell's personality theory", "d": "Beck and Klopfer's model"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) is scored and interpreted mainly through the personality theory of Henry Murray (Explorations in Personality, 1938), his theory of needs and press. Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray introduced the TAT in 1935, and his manual followed in 1943. The set has 31 cards (30 pictures and 1 blank card). For each ambiguous picture the person tells a story with a past, a present and an ending. Henry Murray held that behaviour is driven by psychogenic needs (achievement, affiliation, autonomy and others) meeting environmental press, the forces around a person that help or block those needs; a need and a press combined form a thema. So the examiner finds the hero (the character the teller identifies with), rates the hero's needs and the press acting on the hero. Example: shown a card of a boy looking at a violin, Isha tells of a boy forced to practise by strict parents who then fails his recital. The examiner notes a need for autonomy against a press of parental dominance, with an unhappy outcome. Option a is right because the TAT's scoring categories, needs, press and themas, come straight from Henry Murray's own theory. Exam trap: John Exner, Samuel Beck and Bruno Klopfer are all famous scoring names, but for the Rorschach inkblots, not the TAT. Key note: in practice many clinicians interpret TAT stories qualitatively, and David McClelland's (1953) achievement scoring is also used, but Henry Murray's need-press theory is the basis of the TAT, so a stands.", "topic": "TAT scoring and Murray's theory", "fig": "Henry Murray"}, {"q": "Which of the following personality assessment techniques holds that an individual puts structure on an ambiguous situation in a way that is consistent with his/her own conscious and unconscious needs?", "options": {"a": "Projective technique", "b": "Objective technique", "c": "Subjective technique", "d": "Semi-objective technique"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: projective techniques rest on the idea that a person faced with an ambiguous situation puts his or her own structure on it, and that this structure reveals conscious and unconscious needs. Lawrence K. Frank (1939) coined the term projective methods and stated this projective hypothesis. Because the stimulus (an inkblot, a vague picture, an unfinished sentence) has no single correct meaning, whatever meaning the person sees or tells must come from inside: motives, conflicts, fears and habitual ways of seeing the world. The person does not know what is being scored, so faking is harder than on a questionnaire. The main examples are the Rorschach inkblot test (Hermann Rorschach, 1921), the Thematic Apperception Test (Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray, 1935), sentence completion tests and the Draw-a-Person test. Example: shown a blurred picture of a woman at a window, Pooja says the woman is waiting for a son who will never come home. A clinician treats this theme of loss as a clue to Pooja's own concerns, not as a fact about the picture. Option a is right because putting structure on an ambiguous stimulus in line with one's own needs is the definition of projection in personality assessment. Exam trap: subjective sounds like the natural partner of unconscious needs; but subjective techniques such as the interview and autobiography rely on deliberate self-description, which cannot reach unconscious needs, while only projective techniques use an ambiguous stimulus.", "topic": "Projective hypothesis", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Inkblot test was first developed by:", "options": {"a": "Cristina Rorschach", "b": "Hermann Rorschach", "c": "Henry Murray", "d": "Exner"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the inkblot test was developed by Hermann Rorschach (1921), a Swiss psychiatrist, who published it in his book Psychodiagnostik. Working with psychiatric patients, he tried out many blots and chose those that drew different responses from different patient groups; the published set has ten, and he died the next year, aged 37. The Rorschach Inkblot Test has ten bilaterally symmetrical cards: five black and grey (achromatic), two black and red, and three multicoloured. The person says what each blot might be, then, in an inquiry phase, explains where on the card the image was seen and what made it look that way. Responses are coded for location (the whole blot or a part), determinants (form, colour, movement, shading), content (human, animal and so on) and how often others give the same answer (popular responses). Because the blots are ambiguous, the Rorschach is the classic projective test. Example: Vikram sees a whole card as two people dancing around a fire, which would be coded as a whole-blot response with human movement. Option b is right because Hermann Rorschach created and published the inkblot test. Exam trap: John Exner's name is so closely tied to the Rorschach, through his Comprehensive System (1974), that students take him for its creator; he built a scoring system about fifty years after the test itself.", "topic": "Rorschach inkblot test origin", "fig": "Hermann Rorschach"}, {"q": "Aptitude tests are not designed to measure:", "options": {"a": "Intelligence", "b": "Knowledge", "c": "Ability", "d": "Personality"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: aptitude tests are not designed to measure knowledge already acquired; that is the job of achievement tests. An aptitude is a person's potential, the capacity to learn a skill or acquire knowledge if given training, so an aptitude test predicts future performance in a course or job. The usual line is that aptitude tests measure not what has been learned but what can be learned. Examples are the Differential Aptitude Tests (DAT), with subtests such as verbal, numerical, abstract and mechanical reasoning, and tests of clerical, mechanical and musical aptitude. An achievement test measures what a person has already learned from instruction: a class test, a board examination, a typing test after a course. Intelligence tests are often called tests of general or scholastic aptitude, and aptitude tests measure specific abilities, so both measure ability. Example: before Zoya joins an engineering course, a mechanical reasoning test predicts how easily she will learn the subject (aptitude); her final-year examination measures how much she has learned (achievement). Option b is right because acquired knowledge is what achievement tests measure, the textbook opposite of aptitude tests. Exam trap: students reason that aptitude is not intelligence, but intelligence tests are themselves broad aptitude tests, so intelligence is not what aptitude tests exclude. Key note: aptitude tests do not measure personality either (personality has its own tests, e.g. MMPI, 16PF), but the textbook contrast this item tests is aptitude vs achievement, and acquired knowledge is what achievement tests measure, so b stands.", "topic": "Aptitude versus achievement tests", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Counselling is successful if the process brings positive change in:", "options": {"a": "Belief", "b": "Behaviour", "c": "Emotional distress", "d": "All the three"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: counselling is successful when it brings positive change in all three areas: the client's beliefs, behaviour and emotional distress. Counselling is a professional helping relationship in which a trained counsellor helps a fairly well-functioning person understand himself or herself, make decisions and cope with problems of adjustment. Carl Rogers (Counseling and Psychotherapy, 1942) wrote that its aim is not to solve one particular problem but to help the person grow. Outcome is judged on three linked levels, matching the thinking, doing and feeling sides of a person. Belief (cognition): the client sees himself or herself and the problem more realistically, for example replacing \"I am a failure\" with \"I failed one exam\". Behaviour: the client acts differently, studying regularly, speaking up or avoiding less. Emotional distress: the client feels less anxious, sad or angry and more in control. Example: Tanvi sees her college counsellor after failing an exam, sure she will never pass, crying daily and skipping classes. Counselling has succeeded when she believes she can recover, attends classes with a study plan and feels calmer. Change in only one area would be a partial outcome. Option d is right because successful counselling is defined by positive change in thinking, behaviour and feeling together. Exam trap: each single option is a genuine outcome, so students pick whichever sounds most psychological; when every listed option is a real part of the answer, choose All the three.", "topic": "Outcomes of counselling", "fig": ""}, {"q": "First theory of counselling was propounded by:", "options": {"a": "J.B. Davis", "b": "S. Freud", "c": "E.G. Williamson", "d": "Clifford Beers"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: the first theory of counselling is usually credited to Edmund G. Williamson (1939), who set out trait-and-factor counselling in How to Counsel Students. He built on the vocational matching idea of Frank Parsons (Choosing a Vocation, 1909): know yourself, know the world of work and match the two. Edmund G. Williamson turned this into a full counselling theory, often called the Minnesota point of view. It assumes that each person has measurable traits (abilities, aptitudes, interests, personality) and that each job or course demands certain factors, so good adjustment comes from a sound match. It is directive (counsellor-centred): the counsellor is an expert who gathers data, interprets it and advises. Its six steps are analysis (collecting data from tests, records and interviews), synthesis (organising the data), diagnosis (identifying the problem and its causes), prognosis (predicting the outcome), counselling (helping the client act) and follow-up. Example: a counsellor gives Aman aptitude and interest tests, diagnoses that weak mathematics clashes with his wish to be an engineer, and advises a course that suits his strong verbal ability. Option c is right because Edmund G. Williamson produced the first systematic theory of counselling, the trait-factor or directive approach. Exam trap: Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis (from the 1890s) came earlier, but it is a theory of personality and psychotherapy, not of counselling. Key note: some books give the first counselling theory to Frank Parsons (vocational guidance) or call psychoanalysis the first therapy, but Frank Parsons is not an option and Edmund G. Williamson's trait-factor approach is the first systematic counselling theory, so c stands.", "topic": "Williamson's trait-factor counselling", "fig": "Edmund G. Williamson"}, {"q": "Counsellor differs from psychiatrist in that:", "options": {"a": "He does not have any professional degree", "b": "His focus is more on adolescents' issues and problems", "c": "He does not have basic medical degree", "d": "He works in schools and organizations"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: a counsellor differs from a psychiatrist in that a counsellor does not have a basic medical degree. A psychiatrist is a medical doctor: in India, an MBBS followed by an MD, DNB or DPM in psychiatry. Because of that training, a psychiatrist makes medical diagnoses of mental disorders, prescribes medicines and can give physical treatments such as electroconvulsive therapy. A counsellor is trained in psychology, counselling or social work (an MA, M.Sc. or diploma in counselling), works through talk and the helping relationship, cannot prescribe medication, and mostly helps fairly well-functioning people with developmental, educational, vocational, relationship and situational problems. A clinical psychologist sits between the two: a psychology degree with clinical training and Rehabilitation Council of India (RCI) registration, specialising in assessment and psychotherapy, but also without a medical degree. Example: Dev, a college student, is stressed about his career choice and sees the college counsellor. If his mood became severely low with suicidal thoughts, the counsellor would refer him to a psychiatrist, who could assess him medically and start medication. Option c is right because the medical degree is the defining difference between the two professions. Exam trap: options b and d name clients and settings many counsellors work with, so they sound like differences; but child and adolescent psychiatry is a specialty, and counsellors also work in clinics, hospitals and private practice, so only the medical degree separates them.", "topic": "Counsellor versus psychiatrist", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The core concepts in Rogers' self theory do not include:", "options": {"a": "The organism", "b": "The phenomenal field", "c": "The self", "d": "The belief"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: the belief is not one of the core concepts of Carl Rogers's self theory. Carl Rogers (Client-Centered Therapy, 1951; formal statement 1959) built his theory on three structural concepts: the organism, the phenomenal field and the self. The organism is the whole individual, the place where all experience happens. Its basic motive is the actualising tendency, to maintain and enhance itself. The phenomenal field is everything the person is experiencing at a given moment, conscious or not, as he or she perceives it; for Carl Rogers this private, subjective reality, not objective reality, guides behaviour. The self, or self-concept, is the part of the phenomenal field that becomes set apart as \"I\" or \"me\", the organised picture of who one is, alongside the ideal self, the person one would like to be. When experience clashes with the self-concept (incongruence), anxiety and defences follow, especially when love came with conditions of worth. Example: Aditi's self-concept is \"I am the obedient daughter\", so she distorts her anger at her parents into \"I am just tired\". Option d is right because belief is not a basic construct of Carl Rogers's theory; it is central instead to the rational therapy of Albert Ellis (1955), built on irrational beliefs. Exam trap: the organism and the phenomenal field sound like biology or Gestalt jargon, so students cross them out as not Rogers's own; in fact both are core terms of his self theory, alongside the self, and only the belief is foreign to it.", "topic": "Rogers's self theory concepts", "fig": "Carl Rogers"}, {"q": "The first step of counselling is:", "options": {"a": "In-depth exploration", "b": "Diagnosis", "c": "Rapport building", "d": "Prognosis"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: the first step of counselling is rapport building, establishing a warm, trusting working relationship between counsellor and client. The counsellor greets the client warmly, listens attentively, shows empathy and acceptance, explains confidentiality and what counselling involves. Nearly every model puts this first. Carl Rogers (1957) argued that empathy, unconditional positive regard and genuineness are the conditions for change, and Gerard Egan's skilled helper model (The Skilled Helper, first edition 1975) starts by attending to clients and helping them tell their story. Indian guidance texts give the sequence as rapport building (relationship establishment), in-depth exploration and assessment of the problem, goal setting, intervention, and termination with follow-up. Without rapport, clients hide or soften the real problem, so every later step rests on incomplete information. Example: Farhan, a Class 11 student, is sent to the school counsellor for skipping classes. If she begins by asking why he is misbehaving, he stays silent; if she first chats about cricket, listens without judging and assures him of confidentiality, he soon reveals that he is being bullied on the school bus. Option c is right because exploration, diagnosis and planning all depend on the trust built in the first stage. Exam trap: diagnosis and prognosis appear in the trait-factor steps of Edmund G. Williamson (1939) and in medicine, so they sound like a beginning; but they can only follow once information has been gathered, and that needs rapport first.", "topic": "Stages of counselling: rapport", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Irrational beliefs are replaced with rational alternatives in which of the following techniques?", "options": {"a": "Systematic desensitization", "b": "CBT", "c": "REBT", "d": "Modeling"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: replacing irrational beliefs with rational alternatives is the defining technique of Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy (REBT), founded by Albert Ellis (1955). It began as Rational Therapy and was renamed REBT in 1993. Its ABC model holds that an Activating event (A) does not directly cause the emotional Consequences (C); the Beliefs (B) about the event do. Irrational beliefs are rigid demands (musts and shoulds), awfulising (it is terrible), low frustration tolerance (I can't stand it) and global self-rating (I am worthless). The therapist then actively Disputes (D) these beliefs, asking for evidence, logic and usefulness, until the client adopts an Effective new philosophy (E), a flexible preference such as \"I would like to do well, but I can survive failing\". Example: Siddharth is crushed by a rejection letter because he believes \"I must get every job I apply for, or I am useless\". His therapist disputes the must and helps him replace it with \"I want this job, but one rejection does not make me useless\", and his despair eases into disappointment. Option c is right because active disputing and replacement of irrational beliefs is the core of REBT. Exam trap: CBT also changes thoughts, so it tempts; but in Aaron Beck's cognitive therapy (from the 1960s) the targets are automatic thoughts and cognitive distortions tested against evidence, while irrational beliefs is Albert Ellis's own term. Key note: REBT is itself a form of cognitive behavioural therapy, so CBT is not wrong in a broad sense, but replacing irrational beliefs with rational ones is the defining technique of Albert Ellis's REBT, so c stands.", "topic": "REBT and irrational beliefs", "fig": "Albert Ellis"}, {"q": "Which one among the following is not the criterion for selection of group members for counselling?", "options": {"a": "The subjects have the common goal", "b": "They belong to the same age group", "c": "They have similar, if not identical, problems", "d": "They belong to same community"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: belonging to the same community is not a criterion for choosing members of a counselling group. Group counselling works with a small group (often 6 to 10 people) and a leader, and much of its benefit comes from members helping one another: Irvin Yalom (The Theory and Practice of Group Psychotherapy, 1970) named therapeutic factors such as universality (I am not alone), instillation of hope, altruism and group cohesiveness. So the leader screens possible members and selects people who share a common goal, so that sessions have one direction; who have similar, if not identical, problems or concerns (exam anxiety, say), so that members understand each other and feel universality; and who, especially among children and adolescents, are of about the same age and developmental level. Community, caste, religion or neighbourhood is not required, and people from the same close circle may even hold back for fear of gossip. Example: a school counsellor forms a group of six Class 10 students, including Neha and Ravi, who all want to manage board-exam anxiety; they come from different localities, which helps them speak freely. Option d is right because a shared community has nothing to do with whether a group can work towards common goals. Exam trap: the stem is negatively worded, and same community sounds as if it would help bonding; but homogeneity in group selection means shared goals, problems and age, not shared social background.", "topic": "Selecting group counselling members", "fig": ""}, {"q": "On the basis of IQ assessment the gifted children are those who have IQ:", "options": {"a": "Above 150", "b": "Above 130", "c": "Between 110 and 120", "d": "Below 170"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: on the basis of IQ, gifted children are usually those with an IQ above 130. Modern intelligence tests use the deviation IQ introduced by David Wechsler (1939), with a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15. An IQ of 130 lies two standard deviations above the mean, and only about 2 per cent of people score that high, so it marks the top tail of the normal curve. Common classifications label 130 and above very superior (gifted), 120 to 129 superior, 110 to 119 high average (bright) and 90 to 109 average. Lewis Terman (from 1921), in his Genetic Studies of Genius, followed about 1,500 Californian children with IQs of about 135 or more and found them healthier and better adjusted than average, not frail or odd. Later views add that giftedness also needs creativity and task commitment, as in the three-ring model of Joseph Renzulli (1978). Example: Ishaan scores 136 on a standardised intelligence test, above about 99 per cent of his age group, so he is classified as gifted. Option b is right because an IQ above 130, two standard deviations above the mean, is the standard textbook cut-off for giftedness. Exam trap: 110 to 120 sounds clearly above average and so tempts, but it is only the high-average band; giftedness begins two standard deviations up, at 130. Key note: some older classifications put the line at 140 (following Lewis Terman), but 130 is the standard modern cut-off, so b stands.", "topic": "IQ cut-off for giftedness", "fig": ""}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"jammu-2013": [{"q": "When the researcher himself does not manipulate the independent variable but studies the variations in the independent variable due to natural settings, he is using ______ method.", "concept": "The answer is d: when a researcher does not manipulate the independent variable but studies how it varies naturally, the method among these options is systematic observation. In an experiment the researcher deliberately changes the independent variable (IV) and controls other factors to see its effect on the dependent variable. In non-experimental research the researcher leaves the world as it is and records how variables already differ. Systematic (structured) observation does this with a plan fixed in advance: which behaviours to record, when, where and how to code them, so that different observers record the same thing. It is often carried out in natural settings (naturalistic observation), such as a playground or a classroom. Example: Meera wants to know whether crowding in school buses goes with quarrelling. She cannot crowd children on purpose, so she rides different buses and codes every quarrel on a checklist, noting how full each bus is. Crowding varies naturally; she only records it. Option d is right because systematic observation studies behaviour as it occurs in natural settings, with a planned recording scheme and no manipulation. Exam trap: participant observation and case studies also avoid manipulation, so 'no manipulation' alone does not decide the item; participant observation names the observer's role and a case study names its scope (one person or unit), while systematic observation names a planned way of recording behaviour as it varies in natural settings. Key note: none of the options names 'natural experiment' or 'correlational method', which fit the stem most exactly; systematic observation is the only non-manipulating method offered that studies variables as they vary in natural settings, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "In the experimental method the researcher deliberately manipulates the independent variable and controls extraneous variables to establish cause and effect, the exact opposite of the stem.", "b": "A case study is an intensive, in-depth study of one person, family or unit (for example Sigmund Freud's Little Hans, 1909); it does not study how a variable varies across natural settings.", "c": "In participant observation the observer joins the group's life, as William Foote Whyte did in Street Corner Society (1943); the term describes the observer's role, not the study of naturally varying variables."}}, {"q": "\"Each of us have a potential to be a loving person if only we would recognize it\" explains:", "concept": "The answer is d: the statement that each of us has the potential to be a loving person, if only we would recognise it, expresses the humanistic approach. Humanistic psychology grew in the 1950s and 1960s as a 'third force' against psychoanalysis and behaviourism. Its leaders, Abraham Maslow (1954, Motivation and Personality) and Carl Rogers (1951, Client-Centered Therapy), held that people are basically good, have free will and carry an inborn tendency to grow towards their full potential, which Maslow called self-actualisation. Problems arise when that potential is blocked, for example when love is given only on conditions (Rogers's conditions of worth), so that people lose touch with their real selves. The task of therapy is to help the person recognise and use the potential already within. Example: Kabir believes he is cold and unlikeable. A humanistic counsellor would not dig for childhood conflicts or reward new habits; she would offer warmth, empathy and unconditional positive regard so that Kabir can discover the caring person he already is. Option d is right because the stem stresses inner potential, choice and growth, the core ideas of humanistic psychology. Exam trap: words such as 'potential', 'growth', 'free will' and 'self' signal humanistic; 'unconscious' signals psychodynamic, 'learned' or 'reinforced' signals behavioural, and 'genes', 'brain' or 'hormones' signal biological.", "traps": {"a": "The biological approach explains behaviour through genes, the brain, the nervous system and hormones; it says nothing about recognising an inner potential for love.", "b": "The psychodynamic approach of Sigmund Freud (1900, The Interpretation of Dreams) explains behaviour by unconscious conflicts and early childhood experience, and takes a far less hopeful view of human nature.", "c": "The behavioural approach of John B. Watson (1913) and B. F. Skinner (1938) treats behaviour as learned through conditioning and shaped by the environment, not as an inner potential waiting to be recognised."}}, {"q": "Match the following: I. Structuralism, II. Humanistic, III. Behaviourism, IV. Psychoanalysis. (a) Free will, (b) Conditioning, (c) Introspection, (d) Unconscious.", "concept": "The answer is c: structuralism goes with introspection, humanistic psychology with free will, behaviourism with conditioning and psychoanalysis with the unconscious (I-c, II-a, III-b, IV-d). Each early school of psychology is remembered by one key idea or method. Structuralism grew from the laboratory of Wilhelm Wundt (Leipzig, 1879) and was named and developed by Edward Titchener (1898); it tried to break conscious experience into its basic elements (sensations, images, feelings) using trained introspection, careful self-observation and report. Psychoanalysis, the school of Sigmund Freud (1900), held that behaviour is driven by unconscious wishes and conflicts. Behaviourism, launched by John B. Watson (1913) and extended by B. F. Skinner (1938), studied only observable behaviour learned through classical and operant conditioning. Humanistic psychology, led by Abraham Maslow and Carl Rogers in the 1950s and 1960s, stressed free will and the drive to self-actualise. A quick way to solve match items is to fix the surest pair first: Arjun knows behaviourism means conditioning (III-b), which removes option b; then structuralism with introspection (I-c) removes a and d. Option c is right because it is the only option that pairs all four schools with their defining idea. Exam trap: option b gets structuralism and humanistic psychology right but swaps the last two, linking behaviourism with the unconscious, which Watson rejected outright; match items are built to look half correct.", "traps": {"a": "Option a pairs structuralism with the unconscious and psychoanalysis with free will; the unconscious belongs to Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis, and structuralism studied only conscious experience through introspection.", "b": "Option b swaps the last two pairs, linking behaviourism with the unconscious and psychoanalysis with conditioning, although John B. Watson's (1913) behaviourism rejected the unconscious and studied only observable, conditioned behaviour.", "d": "Option d gets only behaviourism right; it gives structuralism free will, humanistic psychology the unconscious and psychoanalysis introspection, all wrong pairings."}}, {"q": "Incorporation of new information into one's existing knowledge is called:", "concept": "The answer is a: taking new information into one's existing knowledge is assimilation. Jean Piaget (1936, The Origins of Intelligence in Children) described thinking as built from schemas (schemes), organised patterns of knowledge or action such as 'things I can suck' or 'animals'. Adaptation to the world happens in two complementary ways. In assimilation, a new object or experience is fitted into a schema that already exists, without changing the schema. In accommodation, the schema itself is changed, or a new one is created, because the new information does not fit. Equilibration is the drive to balance the two: when assimilation fails, the child feels disequilibrium and accommodates. Example: two-year-old Riya has the schema 'dog: four legs, furry'. Seeing a calf, she points and says 'dog'. She has assimilated the calf into her dog schema. When her father explains that it is a calf that says 'moo', Riya builds a new schema, which is accommodation. Option a is right because incorporating new information into existing knowledge, without altering that knowledge, is the definition of assimilation. Exam trap: assimilation and accommodation are a pair and easily reversed; remember that assimilation changes the information to fit the schema, while accommodation changes the schema to fit the information.", "traps": {"b": "Accommodation is the other half of Jean Piaget's adaptation: the existing schema is modified, or a new one created, to fit information that will not fit; the stem describes fitting information into knowledge as it already is.", "c": "Imprinting is Konrad Lorenz's (1935) finding that newly hatched goslings rapidly attach to the first moving object they see during a critical period; it is early attachment learning, not a process of building knowledge.", "d": "Egocentrism is the preoperational child's inability to take another person's point of view, shown in Jean Piaget's three mountains task; it is a limit of thinking, not a way of taking in information."}}, {"q": "Learning based on consequences of responding is:", "concept": "The answer is b: learning that depends on the consequences of responding is operant conditioning. In operant (instrumental) conditioning, an organism acts on its environment, and what follows the action decides whether it will be repeated. Consequences that strengthen a behaviour are reinforcers; consequences that weaken it are punishers. Edward Thorndike (1898) put cats in puzzle boxes and found that the movements which opened the door and earned food were 'stamped in' while useless movements dropped out, his law of effect. B. F. Skinner (1938) named the process operant conditioning and studied it with rats and pigeons in the operant chamber (Skinner box), where lever presses were followed by food. Positive reinforcement adds something pleasant, negative reinforcement removes something unpleasant, and both raise the behaviour; punishment lowers it. Example: Arjun's teacher gives him a star each time he hands in homework on time, and his on-time homework rises. If the stars stop for good, the behaviour gradually extinguishes. Option b is right because only operant conditioning defines learning by what happens after the response. Exam trap: in classical conditioning the learner also ends up responding, but the response is triggered by a stimulus that comes before it, not shaped by what follows it; 'consequences' always points to operant learning.", "traps": {"a": "Classical conditioning, studied by Ivan Pavlov (1927) with dogs, is learning by association between two stimuli (bell and food); the response is elicited by the preceding stimulus, not controlled by its consequences.", "c": "Insight learning comes from Wolfgang Kohler's (1917) studies of chimpanzees such as Sultan, who suddenly solved problems by restructuring the situation, not by consequences gradually strengthening responses.", "d": "Observational learning is Albert Bandura's (1961) Bobo doll finding that children learn by watching a model; they copied aggression without being reinforced themselves, so it does not depend on the consequences of their own responding."}}, {"q": "How many digits an individual can report back in order after a single presentation as reported by George Miller (1956)?", "concept": "The answer is b: George A. Miller (1956) reported that people can repeat back about 7 plus or minus 2 items in order after a single presentation. In his famous paper 'The Magical Number Seven, Plus or Minus Two', Miller reviewed studies of immediate memory span: a list of digits, letters or words is presented once, and the person must repeat it in the correct order. Most adults manage about seven items, with a normal range of 5 to 9. This is the capacity of short-term memory, the temporary store in the multi-store model of Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968). Miller also showed that the limit is in units, not raw items: by chunking, grouping items into meaningful units, we hold far more. Example: Meera cannot recall the twelve digits 199819472001 after one hearing, but as three years (1998, 1947, 2001) she holds them easily, because twelve items have become three chunks. Later, Nelson Cowan (2001) argued that the true limit, once chunking and rehearsal are prevented, is nearer four chunks. Option b is right because 7 plus or minus 2 is the exact figure in Miller's title and findings. Exam trap: the 'plus or minus two' is part of the answer; options such as 9 plus or minus 2 or 5 plus or minus 3 are built to look like Miller's number.", "traps": {"a": "9 plus or minus 2 (7 to 11) shifts Miller's range upward; it is a made-up figure, and nine is the upper end of the normal span, not its centre.", "c": "9 plus or minus 3 is not a figure from George A. Miller (1956) or any standard memory study; it is a distractor built on the familiar 'plus or minus' format.", "d": "5 plus or minus 3 is also invented; five is only the lower end of Miller's normal range of 5 to 9 items, and even Nelson Cowan's (2001) lower estimate is about four chunks, not 5 plus or minus 3."}}, {"q": "An aroused state that occurs within an individual because of physiological need:", "concept": "The answer is b: an aroused, tense state that arises within an individual because of a physiological need is called a drive. A need is a deficiency in the body, such as a lack of food, water or sleep. The need produces a drive, a state of arousal and discomfort that energises behaviour and directs it towards a goal that will reduce the need. When the goal is reached, the need is met and the drive falls. Textbooks give the chain as need, then drive, then goal-directed behaviour, then need reduction. Clark Hull (1943, Principles of Behavior) made this the centre of his drive-reduction theory: behaviour that reduces a drive is reinforced and learned. Example: Kabir skips lunch while studying. His body's lack of food is the need; the restless, hungry feeling that pulls him from his books to the kitchen is the hunger drive; eating reduces it. Option b is right because the stem names the aroused state, not the deficiency behind it, and that aroused state is the drive. Exam trap: need and drive are a cause-and-effect pair; the need is the bodily lack, while the drive is the psychological arousal it produces, so swapping them is the commonest error.", "traps": {"a": "A need is the physiological deficiency itself (lack of food or water); it gives rise to the drive but is not the aroused state named in the stem.", "c": "Motive is a broader term for any reason behind behaviour, biological or social, such as the need for achievement studied by David McClelland (1953); it is not tied specifically to a physiological need.", "d": "An instinct is an innate, unlearned, species-typical pattern of behaviour, as in William McDougall's (1908) list of instincts; it is a fixed behaviour pattern, not an aroused state produced by a need."}}, {"q": "When a reinforcer works naturally without any prior special training of making contingent for occurrence of any response, it is:", "concept": "The answer is a: a reinforcer that works naturally, without any prior training or pairing, is a primary reinforcer. A reinforcer is any consequence that increases the chance of the behaviour it follows. Primary (unconditioned) reinforcers satisfy a biological need, so they work without any learning: food for a hungry animal, water when thirsty, warmth, sleep and relief from pain. Secondary (conditioned) reinforcers, such as money, praise, grades and tokens, start out neutral and gain their power only by being repeatedly paired with primary reinforcers. Money is called a generalised reinforcer because it is linked with many primary reinforcers. B. F. Skinner (1938) used food pellets, a primary reinforcer, to train rats in the operant chamber. Example: baby Arjun stops crying when fed; milk needs no teaching to work. A gold star from his teacher years later works only because Arjun has learned what stars stand for, approval and rewards. Option a is right because the stem's key words, 'works naturally without prior training', define a primary reinforcer. Exam trap: 'continuous' and 'fixed-interval' sound like kinds of reinforcer, but they are schedules of reinforcement, rules about when reinforcement is given, described by Charles Ferster and B. F. Skinner (1957).", "traps": {"b": "A secondary (conditioned) reinforcer, such as money, praise or tokens, needs prior learning: it acquires value only by being paired with primary reinforcers, the opposite of the stem.", "c": "Continuous reinforcement is a schedule in which every correct response is reinforced (Charles Ferster and B. F. Skinner, 1957); it describes how often reinforcement comes, not whether the reinforcer works without learning.", "d": "Fixed-interval is a partial schedule in which the first response after a set time is reinforced, producing a scalloped response pattern; it is a timing rule, not a type of reinforcer."}}, {"q": "James-Lange theory states that:", "concept": "The answer is c: the James-Lange theory states that emotion follows the physiological reaction. William James (1884) and the Danish physician Carl Lange (1885) proposed it independently. Common sense says we see a bear, feel afraid and then tremble and run. James reversed the order: an emotional stimulus directly triggers bodily changes (racing heart, trembling, running), and the felt emotion is our perception of those changes. In his words, we feel sorry because we cry and afraid because we tremble. Each emotion should therefore have its own pattern of bodily response. Example: Riya hears a loud crash at night. Her heart pounds and her hands shake first; according to James-Lange, noticing that pounding and shaking is what makes her feel fear. Walter Cannon (1927) attacked the theory: the internal organs react too slowly and too similarly across emotions, and cutting their feedback to the brain in animals did not abolish emotional behaviour. Option c is right because the theory's defining claim is the sequence: stimulus, bodily reaction, then emotion. Exam trap: the simultaneous view (option a) is the Cannon-Bard theory, built to oppose James-Lange, so examiners pair them to test whether you know which comes first.", "traps": {"a": "Emotion and bodily reaction occurring together is the Cannon-Bard theory of Walter Cannon (1927) and Philip Bard: the thalamus sends signals at the same time to the cortex (felt emotion) and to the body (arousal).", "b": "Emotion leading to physiological reactions is the common-sense view (we feel afraid, then tremble) that William James (1884) set out to reverse.", "d": "Diffuse arousal not tied to specific emotions is the core of Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer's (1962) two-factor theory and of Walter Cannon's criticism; it contradicts James-Lange's claim that each emotion has its own bodily pattern."}}, {"q": "The concept of primary mental abilities was given by:", "concept": "The answer is b: the concept of primary mental abilities was given by Louis Leon Thurstone (1938). Earlier, Charles Spearman (1904) had found that scores on different mental tests all correlate positively, and explained this by one general factor, g, plus specific factors, s. Thurstone, at the University of Chicago, gave a large battery of tests to students and analysed the scores with his own method of multiple factor analysis. Instead of one dominant g, he found about seven fairly separate group factors, which he called primary mental abilities: verbal comprehension, word fluency, number, space, associative memory, perceptual speed and reasoning (induction). He argued that a person is better described by a profile across these abilities than by a single IQ. Example: Kabir may be strong in number and space but weak in word fluency, while his sister Meera shows the opposite profile, though both earn the same overall IQ. Later work found that the seven abilities themselves correlate, so g returned at the top of hierarchical models. Option b is right because the term primary mental abilities comes from Thurstone's 1938 monograph of that name. Exam trap: Spearman also used factor analysis, so a student who remembers only 'factor theory' may pick him; Spearman's answer was g and s, Thurstone's was several primary abilities.", "traps": {"a": "Charles Spearman (1904) proposed the two-factor theory: one general factor (g) common to all tests plus specific factors (s); he did not split intelligence into primary abilities.", "c": "J. P. Guilford's Structure of Intellect model (The Nature of Human Intelligence, 1967) crosses operations, contents and products into 120 (later 150 and 180) abilities; it is a different and much larger model.", "d": "Jean Piaget (1936) described stages of cognitive development, from sensorimotor to formal operational; he did not offer a factor theory of intelligence."}}, {"q": "When a person is trying to find a large number of solutions which fit certain criteria instead of deducing a single answer to the question, he/she is using:", "concept": "The answer is b: searching for many solutions that fit certain criteria, instead of deducing one correct answer, is divergent thinking. J. P. Guilford (1950), in his presidential address to the American Psychological Association, urged psychologists to study creativity, and in his Structure of Intellect model he separated two kinds of productive thinking. Convergent thinking (convergent production) narrows down to the single best or correct answer, as most IQ and multiple-choice items demand. Divergent thinking (divergent production) branches out to produce many varied answers. It is the core of creativity tests, scored for fluency (number of ideas), flexibility (variety of categories), originality (rarity of ideas) and elaboration (detail). The classic task is unusual uses: 'List all the uses you can think of for a brick.' Example: given that task, Riya lists a doorstop, a paperweight, a step to reach a high shelf, a pestle for crushing spices and a cricket wicket marker, which shows high fluency and flexibility. Ellis Paul Torrance (1966) built the Torrance Tests of Creative Thinking on the same idea. Option b is right because the stem describes generating many fitting answers, the definition of divergent production. Exam trap: convergent and divergent are a mirror pair; converge means to come together on one answer, diverge means to spread out to many.", "traps": {"a": "Convergent thinking, the other half of J. P. Guilford's Structure of Intellect pairing, moves towards the single correct answer, as in solving a sum or a standard IQ item; it is the opposite of what the stem describes.", "c": "In Guilford's Structure of Intellect, evaluation is a separate operation: judging whether information is correct, suitable or consistent; it checks answers rather than producing many of them.", "d": "In the same model, transformation is a product (a change, revision or redefinition of information), not the thinking process of generating many solutions."}}, {"q": "The term IQ was devised by:", "concept": "The answer is d: the term IQ (intelligence quotient) was devised by the German psychologist William Stern (1912). Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905) had built the first practical intelligence test, ordering items by the age at which typical children pass them; a child's mental age (MA) was the age level of the items passed. But a two-year lag means something different at age 5 and at age 15. Stern solved this by dividing mental age by chronological age (CA), giving a quotient, the Intelligenzquotient. Lewis Terman (1916), in the Stanford-Binet, multiplied it by 100 to remove decimals and popularised the short form IQ: IQ = (MA ÷ CA) × 100. Example: Arjun is 10 years old with a mental age of 12, so his ratio IQ is 12 ÷ 10 × 100 = 120. Because the ratio fails for adults, whose mental age stops rising, David Wechsler (1939) replaced it with the deviation IQ (mean 100, standard deviation 15). Option d is right because the quotient and its name come from Stern. Exam trap: Terman is the tempting wrong choice, because he added the × 100 and made 'IQ' famous through the Stanford-Binet. Key note: some books credit Terman because he made 'IQ' the standard term and added the × 100; the quotient itself is Stern's, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Alfred Binet, with Theodore Simon (1905), built the first practical intelligence test and the idea of mental age, but never expressed results as a quotient.", "b": "David Wechsler (1939, Wechsler-Bellevue scale) replaced the ratio IQ with the deviation IQ (mean 100, SD 15); the term already existed by then.", "c": "Lewis Terman (1916) multiplied Stern's quotient by 100 and popularised the abbreviation IQ in the Stanford-Binet, but he did not devise the quotient."}}, {"q": "The method of social psychology that involves asking all group members to identify specific persons within the group they would prefer to have as partners in a given activity:", "concept": "The answer is d: asking every member of a group whom they would prefer as a partner for a given activity is the sociometric method (sociometry). Jacob L. Moreno (1934, Who Shall Survive?) developed it to measure the pattern of attractions and rejections within a group. Each member privately names, say, two or three people they would most like to work, sit or play with for a stated purpose, and sometimes those they would not. The choices are counted and drawn as a sociogram, a diagram of circles joined by arrows. It shows stars (chosen by many), isolates (chosen by nobody), mutual pairs (who choose each other), chains and cliques (small sub-groups choosing only within themselves). Example: Meera, a class teacher, asks each student, 'With whom would you most like to do the science project?' The sociogram shows Kabir chosen by eleven classmates (a star) and Riya chosen by none (an isolate), so Meera pairs Riya with a kind, popular partner. Teachers, counsellors and managers use sociometry to find isolated children and to plan teams. Option d is right because the stem describes exactly the sociometric choice procedure. Exam trap: a sociometric test is filled in like a short questionnaire, so it can look like a survey, but a survey samples opinions on a topic, while sociometry asks every member to choose other members of the same group.", "traps": {"a": "The survey method questions a sample of people about their attitudes, opinions or behaviour, often by questionnaire; it does not ask group members to choose partners from within their own group.", "b": "The interview method is face-to-face questioning, structured or unstructured; it gathers information through conversation and does not map choices among group members.", "c": "The observation method records behaviour as it happens without asking people for their preferences; the stem's procedure depends on members naming whom they would choose."}}, {"q": "\"Personality is formed as a result of social influences\" depicts:", "concept": "The answer is a: the idea that personality is formed by social influences describes socialisation. Socialisation is the lifelong process through which a person learns the language, norms, values, roles and habits of their society and so becomes a functioning member of it. A newborn has inherited tendencies, but who the child becomes (polite or rude, religious or not, competitive or cooperative) is shaped by agents of socialisation: the family first (primary socialisation), then school, peers, religion and the mass media (secondary socialisation). George Herbert Mead (1934, Mind, Self and Society) argued that the self itself is social, formed by taking the role of others, first in play and later in organised games. Example: Riya grows up in a joint family where elders are greeted by touching their feet and meals are shared; at school she learns to queue and take turns; through friends she picks up the slang and music of her age group. Each agent leaves its mark on her personality. Option a is right because only socialisation names the process by which social influences shape the developing person. Exam trap: the other options are also social psychology terms, so they sound right in a 'social influences' stem; ask which one is a process of becoming a person, rather than a single behaviour or evaluation.", "traps": {"b": "Interpersonal attraction is liking between people, shaped by proximity, similarity and physical attractiveness; it is one outcome of social life, not the process that forms personality.", "c": "Prosocial behaviour is voluntary action that benefits others, such as helping or sharing (Bibb Latane and John Darley's bystander research, 1968); it is a kind of behaviour, not the shaping of personality.", "d": "An attitude is a learned evaluation of a person, object or idea (like or dislike); it is one product of socialisation, not the process itself."}}, {"q": "Leadership that is preoccupied with purposes, values, morals and ethics is known as:", "concept": "The answer is a: leadership concerned with purposes, values, morals and ethics is transformational leadership. James MacGregor Burns (1978), in his book Leadership, contrasted two kinds. Transactional leadership is an exchange: the leader gives rewards (pay, praise, promotion) for performance and corrects mistakes. Transforming leadership goes further: leader and followers raise one another to higher levels of motivation and morality, so that followers come to share a purpose larger than their own self-interest. Bernard Bass (1985) developed this into transformational leadership, later summarised as four components: idealised influence (a moral role model), inspirational motivation (a compelling vision), intellectual stimulation (encouraging new ways of thinking) and individualised consideration (attention to each follower's growth). Mahatma Gandhi is often cited as a transforming leader. Example: Kabir runs an NGO. Instead of only paying bonuses for targets, he keeps reminding staff why their work in village schools matters, models honesty, invites their ideas and mentors each one; staff stay late because they believe in the mission. Option a is right because a focus on values, morals and ethics is the defining mark of transforming (transformational) leadership. Exam trap: transactional is the look-alike; it too comes from Burns (1978), but it rests on exchanging rewards for performance, not on values and ethics.", "traps": {"b": "Autocratic leadership, from the study by Kurt Lewin, Ronald Lippitt and Ralph White (1939), is a style in which the leader alone makes decisions; it concerns who decides, not values or ethics.", "c": "Transactional leadership, also described by James MacGregor Burns (1978), exchanges rewards for performance (in Bernard Bass's terms, contingent reward and management by exception); it focuses on tasks and exchanges, not on purposes and morals.", "d": "Democratic leadership, the second style in Kurt Lewin, Ronald Lippitt and Ralph White's (1939) study, shares decisions with the group; like autocratic leadership, it concerns decision making, not moral purpose."}}, {"q": "Which one among the following is not the principle of nonverbal communication?", "concept": "The answer is d: 'use of words' is not a principle of nonverbal communication, because communication through words is verbal by definition. Nonverbal communication is the sending and receiving of messages without words: facial expressions, eye contact, gestures, posture, touch, physical distance (proxemics, studied by Edward T. Hall in The Hidden Dimension, 1966) and paralanguage, the tone, pitch, speed and loudness of the voice. Textbooks list several principles. It is multichannelled: face, voice, body and space send signals at the same moment and are read together. It is culture bound: gestures and display rules differ across cultures, so a thumbs-up or a head wobble can mean different things in different places, although Paul Ekman and Wallace Friesen (1971) found that a few basic facial expressions of emotion are recognised worldwide. It conveys emotions and attitudes powerfully, often more honestly than words, and it is continuous and often ambiguous. Example: Arjun tells his mother 'I am fine' after his exam, but his slumped shoulders, flat voice and avoided eye contact tell her he is upset; she trusts the nonverbal channels over the words. Option d is right because words belong to verbal communication, so their use cannot be a principle of nonverbal communication. Exam trap: in a 'which is NOT' item the odd one out is often the option that contradicts the definition itself; here nonverbal literally means 'without words'.", "traps": {"a": "Multichannelled is a genuine principle: nonverbal messages travel through several channels at once (face, voice, posture, gesture, space) and are interpreted together.", "b": "Culture bound is a genuine principle: gestures, eye contact, personal distance and display rules differ across cultures, even though a few basic facial expressions are universal.", "c": "Conveying emotions is a genuine principle: feelings and attitudes such as liking, anger or anxiety are expressed mainly through face, voice and body, often more convincingly than through words."}}, {"q": "The enduring mental representations of various features of the social or physical world which are acquired through experience and have direct influence on subsequent behaviour:", "concept": "The answer is b: enduring mental representations of features of the social or physical world, acquired through experience and influencing later behaviour, are attitudes. An attitude is a learned, fairly lasting evaluation of an attitude object, which may be a person, group, thing, place or idea, along a like-dislike dimension. The stem's textbook definition stresses three features: attitudes last (they are enduring), they are learned from experience (direct contact, parents, peers, media), and they guide what we later do. Gordon Allport (1935) gave the classic version: 'a mental and neural state of readiness, organized through experience, exerting a directive or dynamic influence upon the individual's response to all objects and situations with which it is related.' The ABC model of Milton Rosenberg and Carl Hovland (1960) splits an attitude into affect (feelings), behaviour (action tendencies) and cognition (beliefs). Example: after years of home-cooked meals and a cousin's illness, Meera holds a firm negative attitude towards junk food: she believes it is harmful, dislikes it and walks past fast-food stalls. Option b is right because the stem lists exactly the defining features of an attitude: lasting, learned and directive. Exam trap: prejudice also fits 'learned and enduring', but it is a special, negative attitude towards a social group; the stem is general, covering any feature of the social or physical world.", "traps": {"a": "Prejudice is a negative attitude (mainly feelings plus stereotyped beliefs) towards members of a social group because of their membership; it is one special case, not the general definition in the stem.", "c": "Socialisation is the lifelong process of learning a society's norms, values and roles; attitudes are one of its products, but the stem defines the mental representations, not the learning process.", "d": "Interpersonal attraction is liking for another person, shaped by proximity, similarity and attractiveness; it is one kind of positive evaluation, not the broad, enduring representations the stem describes."}}, {"q": "Which component of attitude transforms prejudice into discrimination?", "concept": "The answer is c: the behavioural component of an attitude is what turns prejudice into discrimination. The ABC (tripartite) model of Milton Rosenberg and Carl Hovland (1960) divides an attitude into three parts: affective (feelings), behavioural or conative (action tendencies and actual acts) and cognitive (beliefs). Attitudes towards social groups follow the same pattern. A stereotype is the cognitive part, a belief that members of a group share certain traits. Prejudice is the attitude itself, chiefly its negative affective part (dislike, contempt), supported by stereotypes. Discrimination is the behavioural part: unfair action towards members of the group because of their membership. Prejudice stays inside the head; it becomes discrimination only when it shows in action. Example: a landlord believes students from one region are noisy (stereotype) and dislikes them (prejudice). When he refuses to rent a flat to Kabir because of where Kabir comes from, that act is discrimination. Attitudes and actions do not always match: Richard LaPiere (1934) toured the USA with a Chinese couple who were refused service only once, yet most establishments that replied to his later letter said they would not serve Chinese guests. Option c is right because discrimination is prejudice expressed in behaviour. Exam trap: 'emotional' is simply another word for affective, so a and d amount to the same answer, and neither turns feeling into action.", "traps": {"a": "Affective is the feeling component (dislike, fear, contempt); in the stereotype, prejudice and discrimination triad it is prejudice itself, not the step that turns it into action.", "b": "Cognitive is the belief component; towards groups it appears as stereotypes (beliefs about what members are like), which support prejudice but are not behaviour.", "d": "Emotional is just another name for the affective component, so it repeats option a; feelings alone are prejudice, and discrimination requires behaviour."}}, {"q": "The major cause of the greenhouse effect is:", "concept": "The answer is a: carbon dioxide is the major cause of the greenhouse effect among the options. Sunlight passes through the atmosphere and warms the Earth's surface, which then gives off heat as infrared radiation. Certain gases, the greenhouse gases, absorb much of this outgoing heat and send part of it back down, keeping the planet warm, much as glass keeps a greenhouse warm. Without this natural effect, Earth would be frozen. The problem is the enhanced greenhouse effect: since industrialisation, burning coal, oil and gas and cutting down forests have raised carbon dioxide (CO2) levels sharply, trapping more heat and causing global warming. CO2 is the largest single contributor to human-caused warming because it is released in huge amounts and stays in the air for centuries. Environmental psychology studies how people perceive such threats and how to change behaviour, for example by persuading people to save energy. Example: Arjun's school runs a campaign to switch off fans and lights in empty rooms and to cycle to school; each saving cuts the CO2 released by power stations and vehicles. Option a is right because carbon dioxide is the main gas behind the greenhouse effect and global warming. Exam trap: chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs) are famous for an environmental problem too, but their best-known harm is thinning the ozone layer, a different issue from the greenhouse effect. Key note: water vapour is actually the largest contributor to the natural greenhouse effect, but it is not an option, and of the options carbon dioxide is the major cause, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs) are greenhouse gases, but they are present in tiny amounts and are best known for destroying the ozone layer, a separate problem from the greenhouse effect.", "c": "Methane traps more heat per molecule than carbon dioxide, but it is far less abundant and shorter-lived in the atmosphere, so it is the second, not the major, contributor to human-caused warming.", "d": "Nitrogen makes up about 78 percent of the air but does not absorb infrared radiation, so it is not a greenhouse gas at all."}}, {"q": "The Protection-Motivation theory that can be used for designing illness prevention programmes was proposed by:", "concept": "The answer is a: protection motivation theory was proposed by Ronald W. Rogers (1975, revised 1983), an American psychologist, not the counsellor Carl Rogers (1951). The theory explains when people act to protect themselves from a health threat, and why fear alone often fails. Protection motivation, the intention to act, depends on two appraisals. Threat appraisal asks how serious the danger is (perceived severity) and how likely it is to happen to me (perceived vulnerability). Coping appraisal asks whether the recommended action works (response efficacy), whether I can do it (self-efficacy) and what it costs (response costs). People change only when both appraisals are high: a frightening message without a clear, doable solution leads to denial or avoidance. Health campaigns use the theory to design anti-smoking, sunscreen, vaccination and condom-use programmes. Example: Kabir, a smoker, sees a warning showing lung cancer (severity) and learns that smokers his age are at risk (vulnerability). He actually quits only when the campaign also shows that quitting cuts the risk and gives him a helpline and nicotine patches (response efficacy and self-efficacy). Option a is right because the theory is Ronald W. Rogers's, and the option names only his surname. Exam trap: the bare surname 'Rogers' makes most students think of Carl Rogers and client-centred therapy; recognise the theory first, then the name fits.", "traps": {"b": "Hans Selye (1936, 1956) described the General Adaptation Syndrome, the body's three-stage stress response (alarm, resistance, exhaustion); he did not propose protection motivation theory.", "c": "William McDougall (1908) wrote one of the first social psychology textbooks and proposed an instinct (hormic) theory of behaviour; he had no part in health-protection models.", "d": "Kurt Lewin gave field theory (Field Theory in Social Science, 1951) and action research and, with colleagues, the study of autocratic and democratic leadership styles; protection motivation theory is not his."}}, {"q": "The hereditary aspects of personality such as sensitivity, irritability, distractibility, adaptability etc. are referred as:", "concept": "The answer is d: the hereditary aspects of personality, such as sensitivity, irritability, distractibility and adaptability, are called temperament. Temperament is a person's built-in style of emotional reacting: how active they are, how easily they are upset, how strongly they respond and how quickly they settle. It shows up in the first weeks of life, is largely inherited and stays fairly stable, so it is the raw material on which experience later builds personality. Gordon Allport (1961) named temperament, with physique and intelligence, as one of the three inherited raw materials of personality. The classic evidence is the New York Longitudinal Study begun by Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess (1956). They rated babies on nine dimensions, among them activity level, rhythmicity, adaptability, threshold of responsiveness, intensity, mood, distractibility and persistence, and found that most babies fell into three groups: easy, difficult and slow-to-warm-up. Example: baby Meera cries at every new face and startles at small sounds, while her cousin Kabir feeds and sleeps on a regular schedule and smiles at strangers. They differ in temperament long before upbringing could explain it. Option d is right because every quality in the stem is one of the inborn reaction styles that define temperament. Exam trap: trait, type and character are all personality terms, so they look plausible, but none is defined by heredity. A trait is any lasting dimension, learned or inherited; a type is a category; character is the moral, learned side. The word \"hereditary\" points to temperament.", "traps": {"a": "A trait is any relatively enduring dimension on which people differ, such as sociability (Gordon Allport, Raymond Cattell); traits can be learned or inherited, so the word does not single out the inborn reaction styles the stem lists.", "b": "A type is a broad category into which people are sorted, such as Carl Jung's introverts and extraverts or William Sheldon's body types; the stem lists inherited reaction styles, not categories.", "c": "Character is personality judged against moral standards (honesty, loyalty), shaped by upbringing and values; in Gordon Allport's phrase it is \"personality evaluated\", not the hereditary emotional style."}}, {"q": "According to Sigmund Freud if parents take too strict approach during anal stage there is possibility of ______ personality.", "concept": "The answer is b: according to Sigmund Freud, parents who are too strict during the anal stage may produce an anal-retentive personality. Sigmund Freud, in Three Essays on the Theory of Sexuality (1905, with the oral, anal and phallic stages added between 1913 and 1923), described psychosexual stages in which the libido, the pleasure-seeking energy of the id, centres on one body zone at a time. In the anal stage, about 18 months to 3 years, pleasure comes from holding back and expelling faeces, and toilet training is the child's first big clash with social rules. If the conflict of a stage is badly handled, some libido stays stuck there (fixation) and colours adult personality. Harsh, early or punishing toilet training leads the child to hold back in defiance, and the adult becomes anal-retentive: orderly, stingy, obstinate and compulsively neat. In \"Character and Anal Erotism\" (1908) Freud summed these up as orderly, parsimonious and obstinate. Over-lenient training, or a child who defies by soiling, leads to the anal-expulsive pattern: messy, careless, wasteful, sometimes cruel or rebellious. Example: Arjun's mother scolded him for every accident during toilet training; as an adult he colour-codes his files, hates lending money and refuses to change his mind. Option b is right because strict toilet training is the cause Freud gave for anal-retentive fixation. Exam trap: examiners reverse the pair. Strict training gives retentive (holding on); lax training gives expulsive (letting go). The Oedipus and Electra complexes are distractors from the next stage, the phallic stage.", "traps": {"a": "The anal-expulsive personality (messy, careless, disorganised, wasteful) is Freud's outcome of overly lenient toilet training or of a child who rebels by soiling, the opposite of the strict approach in the stem.", "c": "The Oedipus complex is the boy's desire for his mother and rivalry with his father in the phallic stage (about 3 to 6 years), not an outcome of toilet training in the anal stage.", "d": "The Electra complex, Carl Jung's name for the girl's version of the Oedipal conflict (Freud preferred \"feminine Oedipus attitude\"), also belongs to the phallic stage, not to the anal stage."}}, {"q": "According to Jung ______ derives from our prehuman, animal past, when our concerns were limited to survival and reproduction.", "concept": "The answer is c: in the theory of Carl Jung (1875 to 1961), the shadow is the archetype that derives from our prehuman, animal past, when our concerns were limited to survival and reproduction. Jung split the unconscious into two layers. The personal unconscious holds a person's own forgotten and repressed experiences. Beneath it lies the collective unconscious, inherited by all humans from the experience of their ancestors. Its contents are archetypes: universal, inborn images that shape how we react to recurring life experiences such as mother, hero, birth and death. The shadow is the darkest and most powerful archetype. It holds the primitive animal instincts inherited from our prehuman ancestors (aggression, lust, the urge to survive) and the sides of ourselves we refuse to admit. Because we deny it, we project it onto others and meet it in devils, villains and enemies. Yet Jung also saw it as a source of energy, spontaneity and creativity, and wanted it accepted, not crushed, on the way to individuation. Example: Kabir, who prides himself on being gentle, is furious at a colleague's \"aggressiveness\" while failing to notice his own hostile remarks; Jung would say Kabir is projecting his shadow. Option c is right because the shadow is the one archetype Jung tied specifically to our animal past and its survival and reproductive instincts. Exam trap: \"archetype\" is true but too broad, since the shadow is only one archetype among many. The persona is the public mask, and the anima and animus are the opposite-sex sides.", "traps": {"a": "Archetype is Jung's general term for any inherited, universal image in the collective unconscious (mother, hero, wise old man); the shadow is one archetype, so the general word does not name the specific one rooted in our animal past.", "b": "The persona is Jung's archetype of the public mask, the face we show to meet social expectations; it is about social roles, not primitive animal instincts.", "d": "The animus is the unconscious masculine side of a woman (the anima is the feminine side of a man), built from humanity's experience of the other sex, not from our prehuman survival instincts."}}, {"q": "______ traits tell us how people from a particular nation or culture are similar.", "concept": "The answer is d: common traits tell us how people from a particular nation or culture are similar. Gordon Allport (1937), a founder of trait psychology, saw a trait as a real structure inside the person that makes many situations alike for that person and guides consistent behaviour. He separated two kinds. Common traits are shared, in varying degrees, by the members of a culture, so people can be placed on the same scale and compared (how assertive, how hospitable). Individual traits, which Allport (1961) renamed personal dispositions, are unique to one person and come in three levels. A cardinal trait is so dominant that it shapes almost everything a person does (rare; \"Machiavellian\" names one). Central traits are the 5 to 10 outstanding qualities you would write in a reference letter. Secondary traits appear only in some situations (a liking for spicy food). Example: a researcher compares how hospitable Indian students are with students from another country; hospitality there is treated as a common trait. Riya's own blend of warmth, wit and stubbornness is her set of personal dispositions. Option d is right because only common traits are defined by being shared across a cultural group, which is what lets us compare one nation with another. Exam trap: cardinal, central and secondary traits all describe one individual, not a group, and source traits belong to Raymond Cattell (16PF, 1949), not Allport. Cattell also spoke of common and unique traits, but the culture-based definition in this stem is Allport's.", "traps": {"a": "Central traits are Allport's 5 to 10 outstanding characteristics that describe one individual (the qualities you would list in a reference letter); they are personal dispositions, not traits shared across a culture.", "b": "Source traits are Raymond Cattell's deep, underlying factors found by factor analysis (the 16 factors of the 16PF), contrasted with surface traits; they are not Allport's category for cultural similarity.", "c": "A cardinal trait is Allport's single ruling passion that dominates one person's whole life (rare, e.g. Machiavellian); it marks one individual's uniqueness, not a similarity among people of a nation."}}, {"q": "According to DSM-IV-TR classification \"Personality Disorders and Mental Retardation\" are in:", "concept": "The answer is b: in DSM-IV-TR, personality disorders and mental retardation were coded on Axis II. The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, published by the American Psychiatric Association, used a multiaxial system from DSM-III (1980) to DSM-IV-TR (2000). Each patient was described on five axes so that clinicians would look beyond the main complaint. Axis I: clinical disorders, such as schizophrenia, major depression and anxiety disorders, plus other conditions that may be a focus of clinical attention. Axis II: personality disorders and mental retardation, long-standing patterns that begin early and colour the whole clinical picture. Axis III: general medical conditions (diabetes, hypothyroidism). Axis IV: psychosocial and environmental problems (job loss, divorce). Axis V: the Global Assessment of Functioning (GAF), a rating from 0 to 100. Giving personality disorders and mental retardation their own axis made sure they were not overlooked beside a more dramatic Axis I disorder. Example: Riya is admitted with major depression (Axis I); she also has borderline personality disorder (Axis II), asthma (Axis III) and recent unemployment (Axis IV), and her GAF is 45 (Axis V). Option b is right because Axis II was the axis reserved for personality disorders and mental retardation. Exam trap: examiners offer Axis I, which held the clinical syndromes, or swap Axis III (medical conditions) with Axis IV (stressors). Remember too that DSM-5 (2013) dropped the axes altogether and renamed mental retardation intellectual disability.", "traps": {"a": "Axis I listed clinical disorders (schizophrenia, mood, anxiety and eating disorders) and other conditions that may be a focus of attention; personality disorders and mental retardation were deliberately kept off it.", "c": "Axis III recorded general medical conditions, such as diabetes or head injury, that might bear on the mental disorder or its treatment.", "d": "Axis IV recorded psychosocial and environmental problems, such as unemployment, bereavement or housing difficulties, that affect diagnosis and treatment."}}, {"q": "Explaining mental disorders with a combination of theoretical perspectives is:", "concept": "The answer is c: explaining mental disorders with a combination of theoretical perspectives is the multiple causality approach. Early theories were one-dimensional: each blamed a single cause. The somatogenic view looked for bodily causes; it gained strength when Richard von Krafft-Ebing (1897) showed that general paresis, a severe mental decline, followed earlier syphilis. The psychogenic view looked for psychological causes, from the \"animal magnetism\" of Franz Anton Mesmer (1770s) to the unconscious conflicts described by Sigmund Freud and Josef Breuer (1895). Research then showed that almost no disorder has only one cause. The multiple causality (multidimensional or integrative) approach holds that biological, psychological, social and cultural factors act together. Two models express it. The biopsychosocial model of George Engel (1977) asks about all three levels at once. The diathesis-stress model says a disorder appears when an inborn or early vulnerability (the diathesis) meets enough stress. Example: Arjun becomes depressed after losing his job. A multiple causality account cites his family history of depression (biological), his habit of blaming himself (psychological) and his isolation after moving cities (social), all interacting. Option c is right because only this approach explains disorders by combining perspectives instead of choosing one. Exam trap: \"psychometric\" sounds like \"psychogenic\", but it is about measuring abilities and traits with tests, not about what causes disorders.", "traps": {"a": "The somatogenic approach traces disorders to bodily causes alone, such as brain damage or infection (general paresis from syphilis); it is a single perspective, not a combination.", "b": "The psychogenic approach traces disorders to psychological causes alone, such as unconscious conflict (Sigmund Freud) or faulty learning; it too picks one perspective instead of combining several.", "d": "The psychometric approach measures mental abilities and traits with standardised tests (the factor work of Charles Spearman and Louis Leon Thurstone on intelligence); it concerns measurement, not the causes of disorders."}}, {"q": "In which anxiety disorder flashbacks of images, sounds, smell, feelings etc. usually occur?", "concept": "The answer is c: flashbacks of images, sounds, smells and feelings are the hallmark of post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD). PTSD follows exposure to a traumatic event involving actual or threatened death, serious injury or sexual violence: war, assault, rape, a serious accident or a disaster. The person may have lived through it, witnessed it, or learned that it happened to someone close. DSM-5 (2013) groups the symptoms into four clusters. Intrusion: unwanted memories, nightmares and flashbacks, in which the person feels or acts as if the event were happening again, often with vivid sensory fragments (the smell of smoke, the screech of brakes). Avoidance of reminders. Negative changes in mood and thinking, such as self-blame, guilt and detachment. Hyperarousal: being jumpy, irritable, sleepless and constantly on guard. Symptoms must last more than one month; if they clear within a month, the diagnosis is acute stress disorder. Example: months after surviving a bus crash, Meera smells diesel at a petrol pump and suddenly sees the crash, hears the screams and feels her heart pound as if it were happening again. Option c is right because reliving the trauma through sensory flashbacks is the defining symptom of PTSD. Exam trap: the stem calls PTSD an anxiety disorder because it was one in DSM-IV-TR (2000); DSM-5 moved it to a new chapter, trauma- and stressor-related disorders. Its one-month rule is also tested against acute stress disorder.", "traps": {"a": "Panic disorder is recurrent, unexpected panic attacks (pounding heart, breathlessness, fear of dying) followed by at least a month of worry about further attacks; it needs no past trauma and has no flashbacks.", "b": "Obsessive-compulsive disorder involves intrusive obsessions (thoughts of contamination, nagging doubts) and compulsions (washing, checking) that reduce anxiety; these intrusions are not relived memories of a trauma.", "d": "Generalised anxiety disorder is chronic, free-floating worry about many everyday matters for at least six months; it has no single traumatic event and no flashbacks."}}, {"q": "When an individual worries excessively about a variety of everyday problems for at least six months, the person may have:", "concept": "The answer is a: excessive worry about many everyday problems for at least six months points to generalised anxiety disorder (GAD), printed here as \"General anxiety disorder\". GAD is chronic, hard-to-control worry about a number of ordinary events or activities: work, studies, health, money, family. The worry is out of proportion to the real risk, moves from topic to topic and is present more days than not. DSM-IV-TR (2000) and DSM-5 (2013) require at least six months of such worry, with at least three of six symptoms in adults (one in children): restlessness or feeling on edge, being easily tired, poor concentration, irritability, muscle tension and disturbed sleep. Because the anxiety is not tied to any one object or situation, older texts called it free-floating anxiety. Example: for the past year Kabir, a bank clerk, has worried that he will make an error at work, that his parents will fall ill, that his savings will run out and that his son will fail his exams; he is tense, sleeps badly and cannot relax even on holiday. Option a is right because GAD is defined by excessive worry about a variety of everyday matters lasting at least six months. Exam trap: the six-month rule is shared with specific phobia and social anxiety disorder (typically six months), so judge by content: diffuse worry about many things is GAD, fear of one kind of situation is a phobia, and sudden attacks are panic disorder.", "traps": {"b": "Panic disorder is recurrent, unexpected panic attacks that peak within minutes, followed by at least a month of worry about further attacks; it is sudden bursts of intense fear, not steady worry about everyday problems.", "c": "Social phobia (social anxiety disorder) is intense fear of being watched, judged or embarrassed in social or performance situations; the fear is about scrutiny, not about a variety of everyday problems.", "d": "OCD is obsessions (intrusive, unwanted thoughts or images) and compulsions (repetitive acts such as checking or washing); realistic worries about everyday problems are not obsessions."}}, {"q": "Method used by earlier experimental psychologists in which the subjects were asked to examine their own conscious thoughts and feelings and report accordingly, is known as:", "concept": "The answer is b: introspection is the method in which early experimental psychologists asked subjects to examine their own conscious thoughts and feelings and report them. Introspection means \"looking within\". Wilhelm Wundt (1879), who opened the first psychology laboratory at Leipzig, trained observers to report their immediate experience of a carefully controlled stimulus, such as a tone or a flash of light, in short, precise judgements. He kept this controlled inner perception separate from casual armchair self-observation. His student Edward Bradford Titchener used a stricter version at Cornell and named the school structural psychology, or structuralism (1898); his observers broke consciousness into elements: sensations, images and feelings. They had to avoid the \"stimulus error\", naming the object (\"an apple\") instead of describing the raw experience (\"a glossy red patch\"). The method lost favour because different laboratories got different results that no one could check: the Würzburg school of Oswald Kulpe (early 1900s) reported imageless thought, which Titchener denied, and John B. Watson (1913) rejected introspection altogether. Example: in the laboratory, Riya listens to a click and reports exactly how the sound feels in quality, intensity and duration, without saying \"it is a clock\". Option b is right because introspection is exactly this systematic self-report of one's own conscious experience. Exam trap: free association also asks people to report what is in their mind, but it is the clinical technique of Sigmund Freud (1890s) for reaching the unconscious, not a laboratory method for describing conscious experience.", "traps": {"a": "Catharsis is emotional release through expressing pent-up feelings, the \"talking cure\" of Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud (Studies on Hysteria, 1895); it aims at relief, not at reporting the contents of consciousness.", "c": "Free association is Sigmund Freud's technique in which the patient says whatever comes to mind, without censoring, so that unconscious material can surface; it is a therapy method, not the introspection of early experimental laboratories.", "d": "Self-disclosure is revealing personal information about oneself to another person (Sidney Jourard, The Transparent Self, 1964), a concept from close relationships and counselling, not a laboratory method."}}, {"q": "Which among the following is not the basic psychophysical method?", "concept": "The answer is d: \"method of variation\" is not one of the basic psychophysical methods. Gustav Fechner (1860), in Elements of Psychophysics, set out three classical methods for measuring thresholds, the point at which a stimulus, or a difference between stimuli, is just detected. Method of limits (method of minimal changes): the experimenter changes the stimulus in small steps, in ascending and descending series, until the observer's report switches from \"no\" to \"yes\" or back; the threshold is the average switching point. Errors of habituation (keeping the same answer too long) and anticipation (switching too early) are balanced by alternating the series. Method of constant stimuli (method of right and wrong cases): a fixed set of stimuli near the threshold is presented many times in random order, and the threshold is the value detected on 50 per cent of trials; it is the most accurate but slowest. Method of adjustment (method of average error): the observers adjust the stimulus themselves until it just seems detectable or equal to a standard; it is the fastest but least precise. Example: to find Arjun's difference threshold for weight, a tester could raise a comparison weight step by step (limits), present seven fixed weights in random order many times (constant stimuli), or let Arjun add sand to a tin until it feels equal to a standard (adjustment). Option d is right because \"method of variation\" is not one of Fechner's three methods. Exam trap: \"variation\" sounds plausible because all three real methods vary the stimulus; and papers sometimes print the alternative names (minimal changes, right and wrong cases, average error), which are genuine, so do not mistake them for the odd one out.", "traps": {"a": "The method of limits (minimal changes) is a genuine Fechner method: the stimulus is changed in small ascending and descending steps until the observer's response changes.", "b": "The method of constant stimuli (right and wrong cases) is a genuine Fechner method: fixed stimuli are presented many times in random order and the 50 per cent point is taken as the threshold.", "c": "The method of adjustment (average error) is a genuine Fechner method: the observer adjusts the stimulus until it seems just detectable or equal to the standard."}}, {"q": "______ variables involve only whole numbers, units and categories.", "concept": "The answer is a: discrete variables take only whole numbers, separate units or categories, with no possible values in between. A variable is anything that can take different values, and by the kind of values it takes it is either discrete or continuous. A discrete (discontinuous) variable jumps from one value to the next: number of children (2 or 3, never 2.6), number of errors, family size, or categories such as gender, religion and type of school. A continuous variable can take any value along a range, including fractions, limited only by the precision of the measuring tool: height, weight, time and age. A reaction time could be 0.42 or 0.4213 seconds. A quick test: if a value between two neighbouring values is impossible, the variable is discrete. This is a separate property from the four levels of measurement of Stanley Smith Stevens (1946): a count is discrete yet can still sit on a ratio scale. Example: in Meera's village survey, the number of rooms in each house (3, 4, 5) is discrete, while the time each family takes to fetch water (12.5 minutes) is continuous. Option a is right because \"whole numbers, units and categories\" is the textbook definition of a discrete variable. Exam trap: an average of discrete data can be a fraction (2.4 children per family), but that does not make the variable continuous; and continuous data are often rounded to whole numbers (age 20) without becoming discrete.", "traps": {"b": "Continuous variables can take any value along a range, including fractions (height, weight, time); they are the opposite of whole-number and category data.", "c": "Situational variables are features of the setting (noise, lighting, time of day, the experimenter's manner) that may affect behaviour and must be controlled; the term says where a variable comes from, not whether its values are whole numbers.", "d": "Mediating (intervening) variables are internal processes that link the independent and dependent variables, such as hunger between food deprivation and eating; the term describes a causal role, not a kind of value."}}, {"q": "The smallest detectable difference between a starting and secondary level of a particular sensory stimulus is known as:", "concept": "The answer is d: just noticeable difference, difference limen and differential threshold are three names for the same thing, so \"All the three\" is right. When a stimulus changes, we do not notice every tiny change. The smallest change from a starting stimulus that a person can detect is the difference threshold. Because detection varies from trial to trial, it is defined as the change detected on 50 per cent of trials. It goes by three names: just noticeable difference (JND), named from the observer's side, the change that is just noticed; difference limen (DL), from the Latin limen, \"threshold\"; and differential threshold. Ernst Heinrich Weber (1834), comparing lifted weights, found that the JND is a constant fraction of the starting stimulus, not a fixed amount: Weber's law, ΔI / I = k. If the fraction for weight is 0.02, a 500 gram packet needs 10 grams more to feel heavier, but a 5 kilogram one needs 100 grams. Gustav Fechner (1860) built on this to found psychophysics. Example: Riya can tell that her 50 gram letter has become heavier only when 1 gram is added; that 1 gram is her JND, her difference limen and her differential threshold at that weight. Option d is right because each listed term names the same smallest detectable difference, so choosing any one alone leaves out two equally correct names. Exam trap: the absolute threshold (absolute limen) is the weakest stimulus detected at all, starting from nothing; the difference threshold is the smallest change in a stimulus already present.", "traps": {"a": "Just noticeable difference (JND) is correct, but it is only one of the three names; choosing it alone ignores that difference limen and differential threshold mean the same thing.", "b": "Difference limen (DL), from the Latin for threshold, is also correct, but choosing it alone leaves out the other two equally correct terms.", "c": "Differential threshold is also a correct name for the smallest detectable change, but on its own it misses that the other two options are synonyms for it."}}, {"q": "The immediate response in the brain caused by excitation of a sensory organ is:", "concept": "The answer is a: sensation is the immediate response in the brain caused by excitation of a sensory organ. Every experience of the world begins as physical energy: light, sound waves, pressure, chemicals. Each sense organ has receptors, cells specialised for one form of energy (rods and cones for light, hair cells for sound). When energy excites the receptors, they convert it into neural impulses, a step called transduction. The impulses travel along sensory nerves to the brain, and the basic, raw experience that results (a flash of brightness, a tone, warmth) is sensation. Perception comes next: the brain organises and interprets sensations into meaningful objects and events, using past experience and expectation. Johannes Muller (1801 to 1858), in his doctrine of specific nerve energies, added that the kind of sensation depends on which nerve is excited, not on the stimulus: pressing on the closed eye makes you \"see\" light. Example: as Kabir walks into the kitchen, his smell receptors detect molecules in the air and he experiences a sharp, warm smell; that is sensation. Recognising it as his mother's mustard-seed tadka is perception. Option a is right because sensation is the first, immediate result of a sense organ being stimulated, before any interpretation. Exam trap: examiners mix up the steps. Transduction converts energy into impulses; sensory coding is how impulses represent intensity and quality; analysis and localisation are later processing. Illusions distort perception, not sensation.", "traps": {"b": "Sensory analysis is the later breaking down of sensory input into features (edges, pitch, colour) by the nervous system; it follows sensation instead of being the immediate response to stimulation.", "c": "Sensory coding is how the nervous system represents a stimulus's features in neural firing, for example intensity by firing rate and quality by which neurons fire; it is the code, not the basic experience itself.", "d": "Sensory localisation is working out where a stimulus is (where on the skin a touch fell, where a sound came from); it is a further step of processing, not the immediate response to excitation."}}, {"q": "When two lights flash at a certain speed, we perceive singular light oscillating back and forth, this is:", "concept": "The answer is a: when two lights flash in turn at the right speed and we see a single light moving back and forth, this is the phi phenomenon. Max Wertheimer (1912), working in Frankfurt, flashed two lines in quick succession with a tachistoscope. With a gap of about 30 milliseconds the lines looked simultaneous; with about 200 milliseconds they looked successive; but at about 60 milliseconds a single line seemed to move from one place to the other. Nothing actually moved, so the movement could not be built up from the two separate sensations. This study of apparent motion founded Gestalt psychology, whose slogan is that the whole is different from the sum of its parts. The same effect makes films, cartoons and neon signs appear to move. Example: at a railway crossing near Arjun's school, two red lamps blink alternately, and Arjun sees one light swinging from side to side. Option a is right because the phi phenomenon is the name for this perceived movement produced by two stationary lights flashed in succession. Exam trap: Prägnanz, similarity and the figural after-effect are also Gestalt terms, so the Gestalt flavour of every option cannot help; look for the idea of movement. Key note: strictly, Wertheimer used \"phi\" for pure, objectless motion, and seeing one object travel from place to place is now called beta movement; but textbooks use \"phi phenomenon\" for this apparent motion, and beta movement is not offered, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Prägnanz is the Gestalt law of good form: we perceive the simplest, most regular and stable organisation possible; it concerns shape and organisation, not apparent movement.", "c": "The figural after-effect (Wolfgang Kohler and Hans Wallach, 1944) is the shift in where a test figure appears after staring at another figure; it is a displacement after prolonged viewing, not movement between flashing lights.", "d": "Similarity is the Gestalt law of grouping by which elements that look alike (same colour, shape or size) are seen as belonging together; it explains grouping, not perceived motion."}}, {"q": "Which instrument among the following is used for the measurement of reaction time?", "concept": "The answer is a: the chronoscope is the instrument used to measure reaction time. Reaction time is the interval between the onset of a stimulus and the start of the person's response. It is very short, usually about 0.15 to 0.25 seconds for a simple reaction, so early laboratories needed a precise timer. A chronoscope (\"time viewer\") is a clock that starts when the stimulus appears and stops when the subject presses a key. The Hipp chronoscope, a clockwork timer built by the clockmaker Matthaus Hipp (1840s), measured intervals to thousandths of a second and became standard equipment in the Leipzig laboratory of Wilhelm Wundt (1879). Franciscus Cornelis Donders (1868) used precise reaction timings in his subtraction method: the time of a simple reaction is subtracted from that of a choice reaction to estimate how long a mental stage, such as deciding, takes. This founded mental chronometry. Today computers do the same job. Example: Meera presses a button as soon as a light comes on, and the chronoscope shows 0.23 seconds, her simple reaction time. Option a is right because the chronoscope is the only listed instrument built to time short intervals such as reaction time. Exam trap: all four options are classic laboratory instruments, so the test is knowing each one's job; the metronome also involves time, but it beats out a rhythm instead of measuring an interval.", "traps": {"b": "A metronome produces a steady, adjustable beat; Wilhelm Wundt used one to study rhythm and feeling (his tridimensional theory of feeling), but it marks time instead of measuring a response interval.", "c": "A stereoscope (Charles Wheatstone, 1838) shows each eye a slightly different picture so that the two fuse into one image seen in depth; it is used to study binocular depth perception, not reaction time.", "d": "A memory drum is a rotating device that shows words or nonsense syllables one at a time at a fixed rate in verbal learning and memory experiments; it controls exposure time, it does not measure reaction time."}}, {"q": "The ability of brain to merge two images coming from two eyes together is known as:", "concept": "The answer is d: of the options given, depth perception best names the brain's ability to merge the two eyes' images, because binocular depth is what that merging produces. Our eyes are about 6 centimetres apart, so each retina receives a slightly different view of the same scene. The difference between the two images is retinal (binocular) disparity, and it is larger for near objects than for far ones. The brain combines, or fuses, the two images into a single picture and uses the disparity to work out distance. The result is stereopsis, literally \"solid seeing\": the vivid three-dimensional depth that weakens when you close one eye. Charles Wheatstone (1838) showed this with his stereoscope, which gives each eye its own flat picture; the two merge into one scene with depth. Bela Julesz (1960) used random-dot stereograms, which show no shape to either eye alone, to prove that the merging happens in the brain. Example: Kabir watches a 3D film through glasses that send a different image to each eye; his brain merges them and the dragon seems to fly out of the screen. Option d is right because merging the two eyes' images is how the brain produces binocular depth perception, the only listed ability that results from that merging. Exam trap: retinal disparity sounds right because it involves both eyes, but it is the difference between the images (the cue), not the brain's act of merging them. Key note: the exact term for merging the two images is binocular fusion, which is not printed. \"Zipper effect\" is not a standard textbook term for this; depth perception (stereopsis) is the ability that results from fusion, and retinal disparity is only the difference it works from, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Retinal disparity is the difference between the two retinal images caused by the eyes' separation, a binocular depth cue; it is the raw material the brain works from, not the ability to merge the images.", "b": "\"Zipper effect\" is not a standard textbook term for combining the two eyes' images; the merging is binocular fusion, and its result, depth perception, is offered as option d.", "c": "An illusion is a misperception, a percept that does not match the physical stimulus (the Muller-Lyer lines); merging the two eyes' images normally gives an accurate picture, not a distortion."}}, {"q": "Data representing age, weight, height are examples of ______ data, whereas data representing gender, classroom, family size are examples of ______ data:", "concept": "The answer is b: age, weight and height are continuous data, whereas gender, classroom and family size are discrete data. Data are continuous when the variable can take any value within a range, including fractions, so the only limit is how finely we measure. Height can be 152 cm, 152.4 cm or 152.43 cm; weight can be 48.7 kg; age can be 19 years, 4 months and 6 days. Data are discrete when values come in separate steps, either whole-number counts or categories, with nothing possible in between. Family size is 4 or 5 people, never 4.5; gender and classroom are categories, so a student is in Class 8A or 8B, not halfway between. Continuous data are often rounded (we say \"age 20\"), but the variable is still continuous; likewise an average family size of 4.3 does not make family size continuous. The difference matters for graphs: grouped continuous data go into a histogram with touching bars, while discrete categories go into a bar chart or pie chart with separate bars or slices. Example: in Riya's class survey, each student's height in centimetres and time taken to reach school are continuous; the number of siblings and the house each student belongs to are discrete. Option b is right because the first list (age, weight, height) can take fractional values and the second (gender, classroom, family size) cannot. Exam trap: option a gives the same two words in reverse order, so check which list each blank refers to.", "traps": {"a": "This reverses the pair: age, weight and height can take fractional values, so they are continuous, and gender, classroom and family size are discrete, not the other way round.", "c": "Raw data are original, unprocessed scores, and standard scores are scores converted to a common scale (z or T scores); the stem contrasts kinds of variables, not stages of processing.", "d": "Standard and raw describe whether scores have been transformed (z or T scores versus original scores); neither list in the stem is defined by transformation, so this pair fits neither blank."}}, {"q": "In which type of graphical representation the given distribution is first converted into percentage distribution?", "concept": "The answer is c: in a pie chart the given distribution is first converted into a percentage distribution, and the percentages are then turned into angles of a circle. A pie chart (pie diagram, circle graph) shows how one whole is divided into parts. Each category becomes a slice, or sector, whose size is proportional to its share of the total. The steps: work out each category's percentage of the total; convert each percentage to degrees, since the whole circle is 360 degrees and 1 per cent equals 3.6 degrees; then draw each sector with a protractor and label it. It suits categorical data and works best with only a few categories. William Playfair (1801) is usually credited with the first pie chart. Example: of 200 students in Arjun's college, 100 choose psychology, 60 sociology and 40 economics. As percentages these are 50, 30 and 20; as angles they are 180, 108 and 72 degrees, so the psychology slice fills half the circle. Option c is right because converting frequencies into a percentage distribution is the essential first step in drawing a pie chart. Exam trap: bar charts and histograms can also show percentages, but for them the step is optional; only the pie chart depends on it, because its slices must add up to 100 per cent of one circle.", "traps": {"a": "A pictograph shows quantities with repeated pictures or symbols (one stick figure equals 100 students); it is drawn from counts per symbol and needs no conversion to percentages.", "b": "A bar chart shows each category as a separate bar whose height equals its frequency, with gaps between bars; it is drawn directly from frequencies without first converting to percentages.", "d": "A histogram shows a grouped continuous distribution as touching rectangles on the class intervals, with area proportional to frequency; it is drawn from the frequency distribution, not from a percentage distribution."}}, {"q": "Which among the following is not the absolute measure of dispersion?", "concept": "The answer is d: the coefficient of correlation is not an absolute measure of dispersion; in fact it is not a measure of dispersion at all. Dispersion (variability) describes how spread out scores are around the centre. Absolute measures are expressed in the same units as the data (marks, kilograms, seconds). They are the range (highest minus lowest score), the quartile deviation or semi-interquartile range (half the distance between the first and third quartiles), the mean deviation (the average absolute distance of scores from the mean) and the standard deviation (the square root of the average squared deviation from the mean, the most used). Relative measures divide an absolute measure by an average to give a unit-free ratio, so groups measured in different units can be compared: the coefficient of variation of Karl Pearson (1857 to 1936), CV = (SD / Mean) x 100, and the coefficients of range and of quartile deviation. The coefficient of correlation, also Pearson's, is different in kind: it tells how strongly and in which direction two variables go together, from minus 1 to plus 1. Example: marks in Meera's class run from 35 to 85 (a range of 50 marks) with a standard deviation of 12 marks; these describe spread. A correlation of 0.6 between her classmates' study hours and marks describes a relationship. Option d is right because correlation measures the association between two variables, not the spread of one. Exam trap: the word \"coefficient\" links it to the coefficient of variation, which is a measure of dispersion, but a relative one; either way, no coefficient is an absolute measure.", "traps": {"a": "The range, the difference between the highest and lowest scores, is the simplest absolute measure of dispersion, expressed in the original units.", "b": "The standard deviation, the square root of the mean squared deviation from the mean, is the most used absolute measure of dispersion, in the original units of the data.", "c": "The mean deviation, the average of the absolute deviations from the mean (or median), is an absolute measure of dispersion in the original units."}}, {"q": "In a symmetrical distribution Mode is equal to:", "concept": "The answer is a: in a symmetrical distribution the mode is equal to the mean. There are three main measures of central tendency. The mean is the arithmetic average; the median is the middle score of the ordered set; the mode is the most frequent score, the peak of the curve. In a symmetrical, unimodal distribution such as the normal curve, the two halves are mirror images, so the peak, the middle point and the balance point all fall at the same place: mean = median = mode. When a distribution is skewed they separate. The mean is pulled furthest towards the long tail, the mode stays at the peak and the median lies between, so positive skew gives mode < median < mean and negative skew gives mean < median < mode. For moderately skewed distributions Karl Pearson (1857 to 1936) gave an empirical relation: Mode = 3 Median minus 2 Mean. Example: the heights of 1,000 Class 10 students in Kabir's district form a bell curve, and the average height, the middle height and the most common height are all 150 cm. Option a is right because symmetry makes the three averages coincide, so the mode equals the mean. Exam trap: option b is Pearson's genuine formula and looks like the textbook answer, but it is meant for moderately asymmetrical distributions; options c and d are distorted versions of it. Key note: in a symmetrical distribution the mean and median are equal, so b also works out to the mean, but b is the formula used for moderately asymmetrical distributions; the answer the stem asks for is that the mode equals the mean, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Mode = 3 Median minus 2 Mean is Karl Pearson's empirical relation for moderately skewed distributions; it is meant for asymmetrical data, while the plain fact for a symmetrical curve is that the mode equals the mean.", "c": "3 Mean minus 2 Median reverses Pearson's formula; it is not a recognised relation, and in a skewed curve it would place the mode beyond the mean, out in the tail.", "d": "Mean minus 2 Median is not a recognised relation at all; in a symmetrical distribution it would work out to minus the mean, so it cannot be a rule for the mode."}}, {"q": "From the below given sampling techniques choose the non probability technique:", "concept": "The answer is d: purposive sampling is a non-probability technique, because the researcher's judgement, not chance, decides who enters the sample. In probability sampling every member of the population has a known, non-zero chance of being selected, and a random mechanism does the choosing. The main probability methods are simple random sampling, systematic sampling (every kth name from a random start), stratified sampling (dividing the population into subgroups such as gender or region and drawing randomly within each) and cluster sampling (randomly choosing whole groups, such as schools, and studying everyone in them). In non-probability sampling the chance of selection cannot be known: purposive (judgement), convenience (incidental), quota and snowball sampling. Example: Meera wants to study burnout among senior ICU nurses, so she hand-picks 20 nurses she judges experienced and typical. No other nurse had a calculable chance of being chosen, so this is purposive sampling. Option d is right because purposive sampling rests on the researcher's choice of who is relevant or typical, so each member's probability of selection is unknown. Exam trap: systematic and cluster sampling look less random than a lottery, so students pick them; but both begin with a random choice (a random starting point, randomly chosen clusters), so both remain probability methods. Key note: some Indian research texts add a third, mixed family of sampling and file systematic sampling there, because only the first unit is drawn at random and the rest follow a fixed rule; even there it is not classed as non-probability, and purposive sampling is the only non-probability technique offered, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Cluster sampling is a probability method: whole natural groups (schools, villages, hospital wards) are chosen at random and everyone in them is studied, so each member's chance of selection is known.", "b": "Stratified sampling is a probability method: the population is divided into strata such as gender, region or income and a random sample is drawn from each, which makes the sample more representative.", "c": "Systematic sampling is a probability method in standard texts: a random starting point is chosen and then every kth name on the list is taken; some Indian books file it under a third, mixed family because only its start is random, but it is not a non-probability method like purposive sampling."}}, {"q": "A researcher is interested to gain familiarity with the phenomenon or to achieve new insights into it. He is doing ______ research.", "concept": "The answer is a: research whose aim is to gain familiarity with a phenomenon or to achieve new insights into it is exploratory (also called formulative) research. This wording comes from Claire Selltiz and her colleagues (Research Methods in Social Relations, 1959), and Indian texts such as C. R. Kothari (Research Methodology: Methods and Techniques, 2nd edition 2004) repeat it. Exploratory research is done when little is known about a topic. Its purpose is to discover, not to test: it helps the researcher formulate a precise research problem, develop hypotheses, clarify concepts and set priorities for later studies. Its methods are flexible: a survey of the literature, an experience survey (talking to people who know the field well) and the analysis of insight-stimulating cases. Example: Kabir notices that many college students describe a new kind of phone anxiety but finds almost no research on it. He interviews a few students and counsellors and reads widely to learn what the experience is like and which questions are worth asking. That is exploratory research. Option a is right because the stem's two goals, familiarity and new insights, are the textbook definition of exploratory research. Exam trap: descriptive research also studies groups we want to know more about, so it tempts; but descriptive research accurately portrays characteristics that are already defined, whereas exploratory research comes first, when even the right questions are not yet clear.", "traps": {"b": "Descriptive research, in Claire Selltiz's scheme, portrays accurately the characteristics of a particular individual, situation or group (a census, an opinion survey); it needs a clearly defined problem, not new insights.", "c": "Diagnostic research determines how often something occurs or how it is associated with something else; C. R. Kothari groups it with descriptive research, and it too starts from a well-formulated problem.", "d": "Historical research studies past events through records, documents and archives in order to explain the past; the stem is about gaining insight into a poorly understood phenomenon, not about past records."}}, {"q": "Temperature (C, F) is measured on ______ scale.", "concept": "The answer is c: temperature in degrees Celsius or Fahrenheit is measured on an interval scale. Stanley Smith Stevens (1946) described four levels of measurement. A nominal scale only names categories (gender, jersey numbers). An ordinal scale adds rank order (first, second and third in class), but the gaps between ranks need not be equal. An interval scale adds equal units: the rise from 10 to 20 degrees C is the same amount as the rise from 30 to 40 degrees C. But its zero is arbitrary, a reference point rather than the absence of heat: 0 degrees C is simply the freezing point of water. So interval values can be added and subtracted, but their ratios mean nothing. A ratio scale has equal units and a true zero, so ratios make sense (weight, height, reaction time, income, and Kelvin temperature, where 0 K is absolute zero, the true bottom of the scale). Example: Riya says that 40 degrees C in Delhi is twice as hot as 20 degrees C in Shimla. She is wrong: in Fahrenheit the same two days are 104 and 68 degrees F, a ratio of about 1.5, not 2. Option c is right because Celsius and Fahrenheit have equal intervals but no true zero, which is the defining feature of an interval scale. Exam trap: temperature looks like a precise physical measurement, so students choose ratio; only the Kelvin scale, which starts at absolute zero, is a ratio scale.", "traps": {"a": "A nominal scale only labels categories with no order (gender, blood group, jersey numbers); temperatures have an order and equal steps, so they carry far more information than names.", "b": "An ordinal scale gives rank order without equal intervals (class rank, a single Likert item); degrees of temperature are equal units, which an ordinal scale lacks.", "d": "A ratio scale needs a true zero meaning none of the quantity (weight, reaction time, Kelvin temperature); 0 degrees C or F is arbitrary, so 40 degrees C is not twice as hot as 20 degrees C."}}, {"q": "The correlation coefficient between variable X and Y is negative and significant. This indicates that:", "concept": "The answer is c: a negative correlation means the two variables move in opposite directions, so as one increases, the other tends to decrease. The correlation coefficient (Karl Pearson's r, 1896) runs from minus 1 to plus 1. Its sign gives the direction: positive when both rise together, negative when one rises as the other falls. Its size gives the strength: near 0 is weak, near 1 of either sign is strong, and only an r of exactly minus 1 or plus 1 is a perfect, equal-proportion relationship. Significant means the correlation is unlikely to be due to chance in the sample; it does not mean strong, and it never means one variable causes the other. Correlation is also symmetrical: the correlation of X with Y equals that of Y with X, so a correct statement must hold from either side. Example: Arjun finds a significant r of minus .45 between students' anxiety scores and their marks. Higher anxiety goes with lower marks, and equally, higher marks go with lower anxiety. Option c is right because it states the opposite-direction relationship for either variable, which is exactly what a negative correlation describes. Exam trap: option d also describes a negative relationship, but only from X to Y, as if X drives Y; because r is symmetrical and shows no cause, the statement about one variable and another is the complete answer. Key note: \"will lead to\" sounds causal, and correlation does not show causation; read it as \"goes with\", and c stands because a correlation runs both ways.", "traps": {"a": "This describes a positive correlation (both variables rise together), and \"equal proportion\" would mean a perfect r of plus 1; a negative r means the opposite direction.", "b": "This is option a turned round, still a positive and perfect relationship, so it contradicts the negative sign of the coefficient.", "d": "This is a negative relationship stated only one way, from X to Y, as if X causes Y; correlation is symmetrical, so option c, which holds for either variable, is the full description."}}, {"q": "Which among the following is not the basic requirement for a psychological test?", "concept": "The answer is b: subjectivity is not a requirement of a psychological test; it is the very thing a good test tries to remove. Anne Anastasi (Psychological Testing, 1954) defined a psychological test as essentially an objective and standardised measure of a sample of behaviour. Textbooks list the characteristics of a good test: objectivity (administration, scoring and interpretation are free from the examiner's personal judgement, so two examiners give the same score), reliability (consistent scores), validity (it measures what it claims to measure), norms (the scores of a representative reference group, against which an individual's raw score is read) and standardisation (the same instructions, time limits, materials and scoring rules for everyone), plus practicability (reasonable cost, time and ease of use). Subjectivity means the score depends on who does the scoring and this lowers both reliability and validity. Example: two teachers mark Neha's essay answer 6 and 9 out of 10 because each follows a private idea of a good answer; that is subjectivity. A multiple-choice test with a fixed scoring key would give the same score whoever marked it, which is objectivity. Option b is right because subjectivity is the opposite of objectivity and is a source of error, not a basic requirement. Exam trap: the stem is negatively worded, and objectivity and subjectivity look alike; read the question twice and pick the one quality a test designer works to eliminate.", "traps": {"a": "Objectivity, freedom of scoring and interpretation from the examiner's judgement, is a core requirement of every good test, so it is a requirement, not the exception.", "c": "Establishing norms on a representative sample is required so that a raw score can be interpreted (as a percentile, a standard score or an IQ); without norms a score has no meaning.", "d": "Standardisation, uniform administration and scoring for everyone, is required so that scores obtained by different people in different places can be compared fairly."}}, {"q": "A researcher has framed two sets of questions that measure the same concept. After collecting the responses the correlation coefficient between these two sets was calculated to check the ______ of test.", "concept": "The answer is b: correlating the scores on two sets of items that measure the same concept, from one administration, checks split-half reliability. Reliability is the consistency of test scores. In the split-half method the test is given once and its items are then divided into two comparable halves, usually odd-numbered against even-numbered items. Each person's two half-scores are then correlated. Each half is only half as long as the full test, and shorter tests are less reliable, so the half-test correlation underestimates the reliability of the whole test. It is therefore corrected with the Spearman-Brown prophecy formula (Charles Spearman and William Brown, both 1910): full-test reliability = 2r / (1 + r). Example: Kabir gives his 40-item self-esteem scale once to 200 students and correlates their scores on the 20 odd items with the 20 even items. He gets r = .70, which corrects to about .82 for the full test. Option b is right because the stem describes two sets of questions on the same concept, answered at one sitting and then correlated, which is the split-half procedure. Exam trap: internal consistency is the wider family that includes split-half, so it also tempts; the specific method of correlating two sets of items is split-half. Key note: two separate sets of questions built to measure the same concept and correlated is also how parallel-forms (alternate-forms) reliability is described, and split-half is itself one kind of internal consistency; parallel forms is not an option, and the specific method of correlating two sets of items from one administration is split-half, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Test-retest reliability gives the same test twice to the same people after an interval and correlates the two occasions; the stem has two sets of questions, not two occasions.", "c": "Internal consistency is the broad family based on agreement among items within one administration (split-half, the Kuder-Richardson formulas of G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson, 1937, and Lee Cronbach's alpha, 1951); the stem's two-set correlation names its specific split-half form.", "d": "External validity is how far results generalise to other people, settings and times; it concerns the generality of findings, not the consistency of scores."}}, {"q": "The ability of the psychological test to make generalization of the obtained results to other settings signifies ______ validity of the test.", "concept": "The answer is c: the extent to which results can be generalised beyond the study, to other people, settings, times and measures, is external validity. Donald T. Campbell (1957) introduced the term, and Donald T. Campbell and Julian C. Stanley (1963) made the internal and external pair standard in research design. Internal validity asks whether the study's own conclusion is sound: did the independent variable really cause the change? External validity asks whether that conclusion holds outside the study. It has two main parts: population validity (do the results apply to other groups of people?) and ecological validity (do they apply to real-life settings?). Threats include an unrepresentative sample, artificial laboratory conditions and the reactive effects of testing. Example: Ananya finds that a memory-training programme raises recall among college students in a quiet laboratory. If the same gains appear among working adults and among school children in ordinary classrooms, the finding has high external validity. Option c is right because the stem's central idea, generalising the results to other settings, is the definition of external validity. Exam trap: the stem says validity of the test, which sends students looking for a test-validity type (content, face, concurrent); but generalisability is a research-design concept, and none of those test-validity types is about other settings.", "traps": {"a": "Content validity is how well a test's items sample the whole domain it is meant to cover (an achievement test covering the full syllabus), judged by experts; it says nothing about other settings.", "b": "Face validity is whether a test looks, to the people taking it, as if it measures what it claims; it concerns appearance, not generalisation.", "d": "Concurrent validity is criterion-related validity with the criterion measured at the same time (a new anxiety scale against a psychiatrist's present diagnosis); it concerns agreement with a criterion, not other settings."}}, {"q": "Consistency or uniformity of the conditions and procedures for administering the test reflect its:", "concept": "The answer is c: uniformity of the conditions and procedures for giving a test is standardisation. Anne Anastasi (Psychological Testing, 1954) put it simply: standardisation implies uniformity of procedure in administering and scoring the test. Every test taker gets the same instructions, materials, time limits and practice items in similar surroundings, and every answer is scored by the same rules. The second part of standardisation is giving the test, in exactly this way, to a large representative sample whose scores become the norms. Only then is it fair to compare one person's score with another's or with the norms, because any difference should come from the person, not from how the test was given. Example: Rohan takes a reasoning test with 30 minutes and the printed instructions, while Sana takes the same test with 45 minutes and hints from the examiner. Their scores cannot be compared, because the procedure was not uniform. Option c is right because the stem defines standardisation itself: consistent conditions and procedures of administration. Exam trap: the word consistency pulls students towards reliability; but reliability is consistency of the scores (across time, forms or items), whereas standardisation is consistency of the procedure used to obtain them.", "traps": {"a": "Reliability is the consistency of the scores a test yields (test-retest, split-half, alpha); uniform procedure helps reliability but is not its definition.", "b": "Validity is the extent to which a test measures what it claims to measure (content, criterion-related and construct validity); it concerns accuracy, not uniform conditions.", "d": "Objectivity means scoring and interpretation are free of the examiner's personal judgement, so different scorers agree; it covers the scorer, while standardisation covers the whole procedure of administration."}}, {"q": "Scoring of TAT is generally based on:", "concept": "The answer is a: the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) is scored and interpreted mainly through the personality theory of Henry Murray (Explorations in Personality, 1938), his theory of needs and press. Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray introduced the TAT in 1935, and his manual followed in 1943. The set has 31 cards (30 pictures and 1 blank card). For each ambiguous picture the person tells a story with a past, a present and an ending. Henry Murray held that behaviour is driven by psychogenic needs (achievement, affiliation, autonomy and others) meeting environmental press, the forces around a person that help or block those needs; a need and a press combined form a thema. So the examiner finds the hero (the character the teller identifies with), rates the hero's needs and the press acting on the hero. Example: shown a card of a boy looking at a violin, Isha tells of a boy forced to practise by strict parents who then fails his recital. The examiner notes a need for autonomy against a press of parental dominance, with an unhappy outcome. Option a is right because the TAT's scoring categories, needs, press and themas, come straight from Henry Murray's own theory. Exam trap: John Exner, Samuel Beck and Bruno Klopfer are all famous scoring names, but for the Rorschach inkblots, not the TAT. Key note: in practice many clinicians interpret TAT stories qualitatively, and David McClelland's (1953) achievement scoring is also used, but Henry Murray's need-press theory is the basis of the TAT, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Exner's system is John Exner's Comprehensive System (1974) for scoring the Rorschach inkblot test, not the TAT.", "c": "Raymond Cattell's trait theory, built by factor analysis, underlies the 16PF questionnaire (1949), an objective self-report inventory, not a storytelling test.", "d": "Samuel Beck and Bruno Klopfer each built rival scoring systems for the Rorschach in the 1930s and 1940s; neither is used to score TAT stories."}}, {"q": "Which of the following personality assessment techniques holds that an individual puts structure on an ambiguous situation in a way that is consistent with his/her own conscious and unconscious needs?", "concept": "The answer is a: projective techniques rest on the idea that a person faced with an ambiguous situation puts his or her own structure on it, and that this structure reveals conscious and unconscious needs. Lawrence K. Frank (1939) coined the term projective methods and stated this projective hypothesis. Because the stimulus (an inkblot, a vague picture, an unfinished sentence) has no single correct meaning, whatever meaning the person sees or tells must come from inside: motives, conflicts, fears and habitual ways of seeing the world. The person does not know what is being scored, so faking is harder than on a questionnaire. The main examples are the Rorschach inkblot test (Hermann Rorschach, 1921), the Thematic Apperception Test (Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray, 1935), sentence completion tests and the Draw-a-Person test. Example: shown a blurred picture of a woman at a window, Pooja says the woman is waiting for a son who will never come home. A clinician treats this theme of loss as a clue to Pooja's own concerns, not as a fact about the picture. Option a is right because putting structure on an ambiguous stimulus in line with one's own needs is the definition of projection in personality assessment. Exam trap: subjective sounds like the natural partner of unconscious needs; but subjective techniques such as the interview and autobiography rely on deliberate self-description, which cannot reach unconscious needs, while only projective techniques use an ambiguous stimulus.", "traps": {"b": "Objective techniques are structured self-report inventories with fixed answer choices and set scoring, such as the MMPI (Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, 1943) and the 16PF; their items are clear, not ambiguous.", "c": "In some Indian education texts, subjective techniques are methods built on the person's own account (interview, autobiography, case history); they use direct self-description, not an ambiguous stimulus.", "d": "Semi-objective is a type of test item in educational evaluation (short-answer and completion questions), not a personality technique built on structuring an ambiguous stimulus."}}, {"q": "Inkblot test was first developed by:", "concept": "The answer is b: the inkblot test was developed by Hermann Rorschach (1921), a Swiss psychiatrist, who published it in his book Psychodiagnostik. Working with psychiatric patients, he tried out many blots and chose those that drew different responses from different patient groups; the published set has ten, and he died the next year, aged 37. The Rorschach Inkblot Test has ten bilaterally symmetrical cards: five black and grey (achromatic), two black and red, and three multicoloured. The person says what each blot might be, then, in an inquiry phase, explains where on the card the image was seen and what made it look that way. Responses are coded for location (the whole blot or a part), determinants (form, colour, movement, shading), content (human, animal and so on) and how often others give the same answer (popular responses). Because the blots are ambiguous, the Rorschach is the classic projective test. Example: Vikram sees a whole card as two people dancing around a fire, which would be coded as a whole-blot response with human movement. Option b is right because Hermann Rorschach created and published the inkblot test. Exam trap: John Exner's name is so closely tied to the Rorschach, through his Comprehensive System (1974), that students take him for its creator; he built a scoring system about fifty years after the test itself.", "traps": {"a": "Cristina Rorschach is a made-up name built from the real surname to catch hurried readers; the test's creator was Hermann Rorschach.", "c": "Henry Murray, with Christiana Morgan, created the Thematic Apperception Test (1935), a picture storytelling test, not the inkblots.", "d": "John Exner developed the Comprehensive System (1974), the most widely used way of scoring the Rorschach, but he did not create the inkblot test."}}, {"q": "Aptitude tests are not designed to measure:", "concept": "The answer is b: aptitude tests are not designed to measure knowledge already acquired; that is the job of achievement tests. An aptitude is a person's potential, the capacity to learn a skill or acquire knowledge if given training, so an aptitude test predicts future performance in a course or job. The usual line is that aptitude tests measure not what has been learned but what can be learned. Examples are the Differential Aptitude Tests (DAT), with subtests such as verbal, numerical, abstract and mechanical reasoning, and tests of clerical, mechanical and musical aptitude. An achievement test measures what a person has already learned from instruction: a class test, a board examination, a typing test after a course. Intelligence tests are often called tests of general or scholastic aptitude, and aptitude tests measure specific abilities, so both measure ability. Example: before Zoya joins an engineering course, a mechanical reasoning test predicts how easily she will learn the subject (aptitude); her final-year examination measures how much she has learned (achievement). Option b is right because acquired knowledge is what achievement tests measure, the textbook opposite of aptitude tests. Exam trap: students reason that aptitude is not intelligence, but intelligence tests are themselves broad aptitude tests, so intelligence is not what aptitude tests exclude. Key note: aptitude tests do not measure personality either (personality has its own tests, e.g. MMPI, 16PF), but the textbook contrast this item tests is aptitude vs achievement, and acquired knowledge is what achievement tests measure, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Intelligence tests are often described as tests of general or scholastic aptitude, so intelligence overlaps heavily with aptitude; it is not what aptitude tests are designed to leave out.", "c": "Ability is exactly what aptitude tests measure: specific abilities such as verbal, numerical, spatial or mechanical, seen as potential for future learning.", "d": "Aptitude tests do not measure personality either, but personality is assessed with quite different tools (inventories such as the MMPI, projective tests); the classic contrast being tested is aptitude versus achievement, which points to knowledge."}}, {"q": "Counselling is successful if the process brings positive change in:", "concept": "The answer is d: counselling is successful when it brings positive change in all three areas: the client's beliefs, behaviour and emotional distress. Counselling is a professional helping relationship in which a trained counsellor helps a fairly well-functioning person understand himself or herself, make decisions and cope with problems of adjustment. Carl Rogers (Counseling and Psychotherapy, 1942) wrote that its aim is not to solve one particular problem but to help the person grow. Outcome is judged on three linked levels, matching the thinking, doing and feeling sides of a person. Belief (cognition): the client sees himself or herself and the problem more realistically, for example replacing \"I am a failure\" with \"I failed one exam\". Behaviour: the client acts differently, studying regularly, speaking up or avoiding less. Emotional distress: the client feels less anxious, sad or angry and more in control. Example: Tanvi sees her college counsellor after failing an exam, sure she will never pass, crying daily and skipping classes. Counselling has succeeded when she believes she can recover, attends classes with a study plan and feels calmer. Change in only one area would be a partial outcome. Option d is right because successful counselling is defined by positive change in thinking, behaviour and feeling together. Exam trap: each single option is a genuine outcome, so students pick whichever sounds most psychological; when every listed option is a real part of the answer, choose All the three.", "traps": {"a": "Change in beliefs (more realistic, less self-defeating thinking) is one real outcome, but on its own it leaves out change in behaviour and in feelings.", "b": "Change in behaviour (new, more adaptive actions) is one real outcome, but on its own it leaves out change in thinking and in emotional distress.", "c": "Relief of emotional distress is one real outcome, but on its own it leaves out the changes in beliefs and behaviour that keep the relief lasting."}}, {"q": "First theory of counselling was propounded by:", "concept": "The answer is c: the first theory of counselling is usually credited to Edmund G. Williamson (1939), who set out trait-and-factor counselling in How to Counsel Students. He built on the vocational matching idea of Frank Parsons (Choosing a Vocation, 1909): know yourself, know the world of work and match the two. Edmund G. Williamson turned this into a full counselling theory, often called the Minnesota point of view. It assumes that each person has measurable traits (abilities, aptitudes, interests, personality) and that each job or course demands certain factors, so good adjustment comes from a sound match. It is directive (counsellor-centred): the counsellor is an expert who gathers data, interprets it and advises. Its six steps are analysis (collecting data from tests, records and interviews), synthesis (organising the data), diagnosis (identifying the problem and its causes), prognosis (predicting the outcome), counselling (helping the client act) and follow-up. Example: a counsellor gives Aman aptitude and interest tests, diagnoses that weak mathematics clashes with his wish to be an engineer, and advises a course that suits his strong verbal ability. Option c is right because Edmund G. Williamson produced the first systematic theory of counselling, the trait-factor or directive approach. Exam trap: Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis (from the 1890s) came earlier, but it is a theory of personality and psychotherapy, not of counselling. Key note: some books give the first counselling theory to Frank Parsons (vocational guidance) or call psychoanalysis the first therapy, but Frank Parsons is not an option and Edmund G. Williamson's trait-factor approach is the first systematic counselling theory, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Jesse B. Davis introduced vocational and moral guidance into the school curriculum (Grand Rapids, Michigan, from about 1907); he was a guidance pioneer, not the author of a counselling theory.", "b": "Sigmund Freud founded psychoanalysis, a theory of personality and a method of psychotherapy; it is not regarded as a theory of counselling.", "d": "Clifford Beers wrote A Mind That Found Itself (1908) and founded the mental hygiene movement to improve the care of mentally ill people; he proposed no counselling theory."}}, {"q": "Counsellor differs from psychiatrist in that:", "concept": "The answer is c: a counsellor differs from a psychiatrist in that a counsellor does not have a basic medical degree. A psychiatrist is a medical doctor: in India, an MBBS followed by an MD, DNB or DPM in psychiatry. Because of that training, a psychiatrist makes medical diagnoses of mental disorders, prescribes medicines and can give physical treatments such as electroconvulsive therapy. A counsellor is trained in psychology, counselling or social work (an MA, M.Sc. or diploma in counselling), works through talk and the helping relationship, cannot prescribe medication, and mostly helps fairly well-functioning people with developmental, educational, vocational, relationship and situational problems. A clinical psychologist sits between the two: a psychology degree with clinical training and Rehabilitation Council of India (RCI) registration, specialising in assessment and psychotherapy, but also without a medical degree. Example: Dev, a college student, is stressed about his career choice and sees the college counsellor. If his mood became severely low with suicidal thoughts, the counsellor would refer him to a psychiatrist, who could assess him medically and start medication. Option c is right because the medical degree is the defining difference between the two professions. Exam trap: options b and d name clients and settings many counsellors work with, so they sound like differences; but child and adolescent psychiatry is a specialty, and counsellors also work in clinics, hospitals and private practice, so only the medical degree separates them.", "traps": {"a": "This is false: a counsellor holds a professional qualification in psychology, counselling or social work; what the counsellor lacks is a medical degree.", "b": "Many counsellors work with adolescents, but so do child and adolescent psychiatrists, and counsellors also help adults, couples and older people; age group is not the defining difference.", "d": "Schools and organisations are common counselling settings, but counsellors also work in clinics, hospitals and private practice; the workplace does not define the profession."}}, {"q": "The core concepts in Rogers' self theory do not include:", "concept": "The answer is d: the belief is not one of the core concepts of Carl Rogers's self theory. Carl Rogers (Client-Centered Therapy, 1951; formal statement 1959) built his theory on three structural concepts: the organism, the phenomenal field and the self. The organism is the whole individual, the place where all experience happens. Its basic motive is the actualising tendency, to maintain and enhance itself. The phenomenal field is everything the person is experiencing at a given moment, conscious or not, as he or she perceives it; for Carl Rogers this private, subjective reality, not objective reality, guides behaviour. The self, or self-concept, is the part of the phenomenal field that becomes set apart as \"I\" or \"me\", the organised picture of who one is, alongside the ideal self, the person one would like to be. When experience clashes with the self-concept (incongruence), anxiety and defences follow, especially when love came with conditions of worth. Example: Aditi's self-concept is \"I am the obedient daughter\", so she distorts her anger at her parents into \"I am just tired\". Option d is right because belief is not a basic construct of Carl Rogers's theory; it is central instead to the rational therapy of Albert Ellis (1955), built on irrational beliefs. Exam trap: the organism and the phenomenal field sound like biology or Gestalt jargon, so students cross them out as not Rogers's own; in fact both are core terms of his self theory, alongside the self, and only the belief is foreign to it.", "traps": {"a": "The organism, the whole individual and the locus of all experience, with its actualising tendency, is one of Carl Rogers's three core concepts.", "b": "The phenomenal field, the person's total subjective experience of the world, is a core concept: for Carl Rogers behaviour follows the world as it is perceived.", "c": "The self, the \"I\" or \"me\" part of the phenomenal field (the self-concept), is the central concept that gives self theory its name."}}, {"q": "The first step of counselling is:", "concept": "The answer is c: the first step of counselling is rapport building, establishing a warm, trusting working relationship between counsellor and client. The counsellor greets the client warmly, listens attentively, shows empathy and acceptance, explains confidentiality and what counselling involves. Nearly every model puts this first. Carl Rogers (1957) argued that empathy, unconditional positive regard and genuineness are the conditions for change, and Gerard Egan's skilled helper model (The Skilled Helper, first edition 1975) starts by attending to clients and helping them tell their story. Indian guidance texts give the sequence as rapport building (relationship establishment), in-depth exploration and assessment of the problem, goal setting, intervention, and termination with follow-up. Without rapport, clients hide or soften the real problem, so every later step rests on incomplete information. Example: Farhan, a Class 11 student, is sent to the school counsellor for skipping classes. If she begins by asking why he is misbehaving, he stays silent; if she first chats about cricket, listens without judging and assures him of confidentiality, he soon reveals that he is being bullied on the school bus. Option c is right because exploration, diagnosis and planning all depend on the trust built in the first stage. Exam trap: diagnosis and prognosis appear in the trait-factor steps of Edmund G. Williamson (1939) and in medicine, so they sound like a beginning; but they can only follow once information has been gathered, and that needs rapport first.", "traps": {"a": "In-depth exploration of the problem is the second stage; a client will not explore painful material openly until rapport and trust exist.", "b": "Diagnosis, identifying the problem and its causes, is a step in Edmund G. Williamson's trait-factor model and in medicine, but it comes after data gathering and exploration, not first.", "d": "Prognosis, predicting the likely outcome, follows diagnosis in Edmund G. Williamson's sequence; it cannot come first, because nothing is yet known about the problem."}}, {"q": "Irrational beliefs are replaced with rational alternatives in which of the following techniques?", "concept": "The answer is c: replacing irrational beliefs with rational alternatives is the defining technique of Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy (REBT), founded by Albert Ellis (1955). It began as Rational Therapy and was renamed REBT in 1993. Its ABC model holds that an Activating event (A) does not directly cause the emotional Consequences (C); the Beliefs (B) about the event do. Irrational beliefs are rigid demands (musts and shoulds), awfulising (it is terrible), low frustration tolerance (I can't stand it) and global self-rating (I am worthless). The therapist then actively Disputes (D) these beliefs, asking for evidence, logic and usefulness, until the client adopts an Effective new philosophy (E), a flexible preference such as \"I would like to do well, but I can survive failing\". Example: Siddharth is crushed by a rejection letter because he believes \"I must get every job I apply for, or I am useless\". His therapist disputes the must and helps him replace it with \"I want this job, but one rejection does not make me useless\", and his despair eases into disappointment. Option c is right because active disputing and replacement of irrational beliefs is the core of REBT. Exam trap: CBT also changes thoughts, so it tempts; but in Aaron Beck's cognitive therapy (from the 1960s) the targets are automatic thoughts and cognitive distortions tested against evidence, while irrational beliefs is Albert Ellis's own term. Key note: REBT is itself a form of cognitive behavioural therapy, so CBT is not wrong in a broad sense, but replacing irrational beliefs with rational ones is the defining technique of Albert Ellis's REBT, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Systematic desensitisation (Joseph Wolpe, 1958) pairs deep relaxation with a graded hierarchy of feared situations to remove anxiety; it changes conditioned fear, not beliefs.", "b": "CBT, especially Aaron Beck's cognitive therapy, also changes thinking, but it targets automatic thoughts and cognitive distortions tested like hypotheses; replacing irrational beliefs through disputation is REBT's own technique.", "d": "Modelling (Albert Bandura, whose Bobo doll study appeared in 1961) teaches new behaviour by having the client watch someone perform it; it works through observation, not by disputing beliefs."}}, {"q": "Which one among the following is not the criterion for selection of group members for counselling?", "concept": "The answer is d: belonging to the same community is not a criterion for choosing members of a counselling group. Group counselling works with a small group (often 6 to 10 people) and a leader, and much of its benefit comes from members helping one another: Irvin Yalom (The Theory and Practice of Group Psychotherapy, 1970) named therapeutic factors such as universality (I am not alone), instillation of hope, altruism and group cohesiveness. So the leader screens possible members and selects people who share a common goal, so that sessions have one direction; who have similar, if not identical, problems or concerns (exam anxiety, say), so that members understand each other and feel universality; and who, especially among children and adolescents, are of about the same age and developmental level. Community, caste, religion or neighbourhood is not required, and people from the same close circle may even hold back for fear of gossip. Example: a school counsellor forms a group of six Class 10 students, including Neha and Ravi, who all want to manage board-exam anxiety; they come from different localities, which helps them speak freely. Option d is right because a shared community has nothing to do with whether a group can work towards common goals. Exam trap: the stem is negatively worded, and same community sounds as if it would help bonding; but homogeneity in group selection means shared goals, problems and age, not shared social background.", "traps": {"a": "A common goal is a main selection criterion: members working towards the same aim give the group direction and cohesion.", "b": "A similar age group is a standard criterion, especially for children and adolescents, so that concerns, maturity and language match.", "c": "Similar, if not identical, problems is a standard criterion: it creates universality, the sense that one is not alone, which Irvin Yalom counted among the therapeutic factors."}}, {"q": "On the basis of IQ assessment the gifted children are those who have IQ:", "concept": "The answer is b: on the basis of IQ, gifted children are usually those with an IQ above 130. Modern intelligence tests use the deviation IQ introduced by David Wechsler (1939), with a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15. An IQ of 130 lies two standard deviations above the mean, and only about 2 per cent of people score that high, so it marks the top tail of the normal curve. Common classifications label 130 and above very superior (gifted), 120 to 129 superior, 110 to 119 high average (bright) and 90 to 109 average. Lewis Terman (from 1921), in his Genetic Studies of Genius, followed about 1,500 Californian children with IQs of about 135 or more and found them healthier and better adjusted than average, not frail or odd. Later views add that giftedness also needs creativity and task commitment, as in the three-ring model of Joseph Renzulli (1978). Example: Ishaan scores 136 on a standardised intelligence test, above about 99 per cent of his age group, so he is classified as gifted. Option b is right because an IQ above 130, two standard deviations above the mean, is the standard textbook cut-off for giftedness. Exam trap: 110 to 120 sounds clearly above average and so tempts, but it is only the high-average band; giftedness begins two standard deviations up, at 130. Key note: some older classifications put the line at 140 (following Lewis Terman), but 130 is the standard modern cut-off, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Above 150 is far rarer (well under 1 in 1,000 people) and is sometimes called highly or exceptionally gifted; it is not the usual cut-off for giftedness.", "c": "110 to 120 is the high-average or bright range, under one and a half standard deviations above the mean; these children are above average but not gifted.", "d": "Below 170 is not a cut-off at all: it would include almost everyone, from children with intellectual disability to the gifted."}}]});
